WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:11.309
Right, I do not detect any surge of energy
around the Board on that front, so let us

00:00:11.309 --> 00:00:15.330
capture the energy and take it on to the next
item on the agenda, which is the allocations.

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:20.990
Before I invite you, Paul, to introduce the
paper, can I just remind the Board of its

00:00:20.990 --> 00:00:24.730
governance responsibilities in relation to
this?

00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:30.939
This is an exercise that we are about to undertake
of enormous magnitude and complexity, with

00:00:30.939 --> 00:00:36.040
very substantial implications for the population
of England, and I would ask the Board to bear

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:42.780
that in mind; to bear in mind also that nowhere
else in the world would an independent board

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:47.640
be capable, be invited or be empowered to
take the decisions that we are being asked

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:52.550
to take today, and that carries with it an
enormous responsibility.

00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:57.120
This is a decision or a set of decisions that
are to be taken without any party-political

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:02.960
input, but looking entirely within the framework
of the Five Year Forward View and the other

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:06.670
principles that Simon has just outlined.

00:01:06.670 --> 00:01:10.510
In terms of the governance – and I come
back to this deliberately – I just want

00:01:10.510 --> 00:01:16.120
to emphasise and to remind the Board that
we are not coming to the exercise cold this

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:17.120
morning.

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:20.690
This is not a sort of traditional exercise
in which the executive side present proposals

00:01:20.690 --> 00:01:24.930
to the non-execs and the Board somehow stumbles
towards a conclusion.

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:29.760
This is an exercise which has involved every
member of this Board: in the early development

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:34.190
of the thinking of the strategy – remember,
this is the fourth year in which we have done

00:01:34.190 --> 00:01:40.330
this, an allocations exercise – in trying
to factor the implications of the Five Year

00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:46.260
Forward View and the stabilisation to which
Simon has referred into the allocations; we

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:51.440
have had some early development strategic
meetings of the Board with the executive team,

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:56.300
in which the Board has given the executive
team some steers against these criteria.

00:01:56.300 --> 00:02:01.421
On Monday this week, the Commissioning Committee
formally considered the draft paper as it

00:02:01.421 --> 00:02:06.790
stood at that stage, and we have taken on
Board input from every member of the Board,

00:02:06.790 --> 00:02:10.780
and there has been a subsequent set of iterations.

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:17.170
This is a multi-dimensional, complicated exercise,
and we as a Board need to be satisfied at

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:24.060
the end of this set of deliberations that,
one: we all understand the implications of

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:29.469
what these decisions are for the five Year
Forward View and for our other criteria, and

00:02:29.469 --> 00:02:31.590
be content to sign them off.

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:37.329
There are some options which we will be invited
to address in the paper, and I would hope

00:02:37.329 --> 00:02:43.139
that by the end of our deliberations we will
have come to a point where we can assure ourselves

00:02:43.139 --> 00:02:48.819
that what we are doing is in the best interests
of carrying forward our commitments and our

00:02:48.819 --> 00:02:49.819
strategy.

00:02:49.819 --> 00:02:53.950
On that note, Paul, may I invite you to introduce
the paper, please?

00:02:53.950 --> 00:02:56.160
Thank you, Malcolm.

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:00.680
Welcome to the first stage of the journey
that we began back in 2013.

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:05.269
We accelerated that journey in 2014, and I
think I would say that this particular leg

00:03:05.269 --> 00:03:11.099
in 2015 is – if you can have a bigger leg
than other legs, I am not sure – the biggest

00:03:11.099 --> 00:03:14.430
leg, and certainly the one which will have
the biggest impact.

00:03:14.430 --> 00:03:18.489
It is a journey, of course, towards fairer
allocations, giving every health economy the

00:03:18.489 --> 00:03:22.739
best possible chance of succeeding in the
challenging times we are going through.

00:03:22.739 --> 00:03:27.190
It is also a journey to use allocations to
support the transformational strategy for

00:03:27.190 --> 00:03:32.260
the NHS, which will have profound implications
for how we invest our money.

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:36.849
I make no apology for the fact that this is
a fairly complex set of proposals, because

00:03:36.849 --> 00:03:43.260
we are having to wrestle with a whole series
of ‘and/and’ propositions; competing priorities

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:46.079
which need to be reconciled.

00:03:46.079 --> 00:03:50.790
For example, the stabilisation that you have
talked about, Malcolm, of the NHS, and the

00:03:50.790 --> 00:03:56.430
need to make the Forward View happen as quickly
as possible; for example, sorting out underfunded

00:03:56.430 --> 00:04:02.249
CCGs in particular, and wider health economies,
at the same time as not completely destabilising

00:04:02.249 --> 00:04:04.829
those which are currently overfunded.

00:04:04.829 --> 00:04:10.499
Moving to fairer allocations, closely aligned
to the ever-improving set of target allocations

00:04:10.499 --> 00:04:15.340
we have developed, and at the same time focusing,
as we are required to do, on the needs of

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:17.209
the most deprived communities.

00:04:17.209 --> 00:04:22.900
Finally, holistic, place-based allocations,
which is very much the theme of where we are

00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:27.330
trying to get to in the Health Service at
the moment, but at the same time having to

00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:31.949
have realistic allocations for individual
organisations to manage to in the years ahead.

00:04:31.949 --> 00:04:38.229
There is a whole series of interesting polarities
that we need to reconcile in these proposals.

00:04:38.229 --> 00:04:43.780
Our crucial task, obviously today, is to deploy
£560 billion – that trips off the tongue

00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:50.460
– of precious taxpayers’ money in a way
which demonstrably maximises impact on the

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:55.030
pace of progress towards the Five Year Forward
View, which of course is what unlocked the

00:04:55.030 --> 00:04:59.740
relative fiscal generosity of the settlement
for the NHS.

00:04:59.740 --> 00:05:04.099
As Malcolm has said, we need to make decisions
today which will stand the test of time, especially

00:05:04.099 --> 00:05:09.860
because this time round we are making choices
for the next five years rather than just for

00:05:09.860 --> 00:05:12.830
the next year or possibly two.

00:05:12.830 --> 00:05:17.340
We need to make sure we get these right so
that every health community has the chance

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:22.680
of being successful in the one-off opportunity
it now has to shape its future in the five-year

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:25.930
planning processes which we will be talking
about later today.

00:05:25.930 --> 00:05:30.750
It is worth perhaps spending a few minutes
over the paper as we go through.

00:05:30.750 --> 00:05:35.120
Before moving on, I would just like to thank
the Allocations Steering Group which brings

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:40.169
together CCG colleagues and NHS England regional
and central teams, and indeed the Finance

00:05:40.169 --> 00:05:45.430
Working Group that we run with the NHS Clinical
Commissioners Group, both of which have contributed

00:05:45.430 --> 00:05:48.379
significantly to the proposals you have in
front of you.

00:05:48.379 --> 00:05:53.009
I should say, as a measure of the commitment
of those two groups: I had an email from a

00:05:53.009 --> 00:05:57.629
very hardworking GP and clinical leader from
Swindon this morning, Peter Crouch – at

00:05:57.629 --> 00:06:03.090
01.43 this morning, to be more precise – with
his confirmation that he had been through

00:06:03.090 --> 00:06:07.120
all of the numbers that I am about to present,
and despite testing them to destruction had

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:11.270
not managed to find anything which was particularly
suspect.

00:06:11.270 --> 00:06:15.009
Thanks to Peter and to the whole of those
groups for making my life more interesting

00:06:15.009 --> 00:06:20.400
by emails at 01.43, which I have responded
to but not necessarily at 01.43.

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:25.039
Also, thank you to the Allocations team, who
have done a miraculous job of converting the

00:06:25.039 --> 00:06:31.379
settlement achieved at literally 23.55 on
24th November into a full-blown multi-year,

00:06:31.379 --> 00:06:35.160
multi strand package in 15 action-packed days
and nights.

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:39.979
Some of the team are here; many of them are
resting from that experience, no doubt.

00:06:39.979 --> 00:06:42.669
So much for preamble.

00:06:42.669 --> 00:06:46.210
If you turn to the paper, which you should
have; I hope the members of the public who

00:06:46.210 --> 00:06:51.120
are in the room have access to the papers
that have come round, and it should be on

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:53.250
the website as well at this point.

00:06:53.250 --> 00:06:59.349
I am just going to canter through as best
I can a long-ish paper, but I will do it in

00:06:59.349 --> 00:07:04.590
three main legs to give you a chance to recover
in between.

00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:07.930
Simon has already spoilt my first bit, as
always, which is the main things we are trying

00:07:07.930 --> 00:07:08.930
to achieve.

00:07:08.930 --> 00:07:11.940
They are set out under the summary of benefits
of the package in the cover note.

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:15.610
I am not going to re-rehearse those; they
will come back as the paper goes through.

00:07:15.610 --> 00:07:19.810
Obviously, finance directors are more thorough
than chief executives, so I have a fourth,

00:07:19.810 --> 00:07:24.110
which you see at the bottom of that list,
which is to incentivise and encourage and

00:07:24.110 --> 00:07:30.949
support the long-term collaboration between
commissioners and providers; in particular,

00:07:30.949 --> 00:07:35.000
around the planning processes that we are
going to be talking about.

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:38.010
We are trying to find ways – and you will
see the proposals at the back of the paper

00:07:38.010 --> 00:07:45.259
– to use the allocations, to use this opportunity
of five year allocations, to encourage shared

00:07:45.259 --> 00:07:49.960
strategic planning, supported by both visibility
of the projected commissioning resources that

00:07:49.960 --> 00:07:55.330
you can see there, and some incentives around
joint planning related to the allocations

00:07:55.330 --> 00:07:56.330
themselves.

00:07:56.330 --> 00:08:02.340
We are trying at the same time to find the
opportunities to pilot things like shared

00:08:02.340 --> 00:08:07.060
financial control totals, which are not directly
related to the allocations, but I think are

00:08:07.060 --> 00:08:12.520
an important of the suite of things that we
can use to move forward in a rather more joined-up

00:08:12.520 --> 00:08:13.520
fashion.

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:17.340
However, I will come back to that if we survive
the next half an hour or so for further discussion

00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:20.000
when we get to the back-end of the paper.

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:24.850
In the main paper then, you can see set out
on page 3 the settlement as it has come through.

00:08:24.850 --> 00:08:27.680
Simon has already rehearsed its main facets.

00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:31.330
You can see in the middle of it the £3.8
billion for 2016/2017.

00:08:31.330 --> 00:08:35.190
You can see that there is another healthy
slug of real-terms growth in 2017/2018.

00:08:35.190 --> 00:08:39.330
We then have a couple of years where we are
more reliant on our own efficiencies coming

00:08:39.330 --> 00:08:42.410
through to generate the capacity to invest.

00:08:42.410 --> 00:08:47.510
Then in 2020/2021, the Chancellor throws off
his cloak of austerity and we all go to a

00:08:47.510 --> 00:08:52.820
slightly more generous regime in 2020/2021,
when we can continue to increase the pace

00:08:52.820 --> 00:08:54.910
of transformation.

00:08:54.910 --> 00:08:59.769
I would stress that this is real money for
the NHS; it is real money that comes from

00:08:59.769 --> 00:09:04.991
a combination of both the Chancellor and the
Treasury, and wider government, and from the

00:09:04.991 --> 00:09:09.790
significant savings that the DH, the Department
of Health, is making as a contribution to

00:09:09.790 --> 00:09:10.790
generating them.

00:09:10.790 --> 00:09:15.560
However, for us it is real, new money that
will come through, and it is real growth in

00:09:15.560 --> 00:09:21.360
addition to real-terms protection, obviously,
for inflation, which is on top of the numbers

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:23.750
that you can see there.

00:09:23.750 --> 00:09:27.769
It comes in two phases; or rather, the priorities
for what we do with it come in two phases,

00:09:27.769 --> 00:09:31.860
and I think it is really important that we
have that clear in our minds as we think about

00:09:31.860 --> 00:09:33.090
the allocations.

00:09:33.090 --> 00:09:38.250
The first thing is, in 2016/2017, as I will
explain, for a variety of reasons the priority

00:09:38.250 --> 00:09:45.120
has to be to re-float, to re-stabilise, to
correct a number of the financial imbalances

00:09:45.120 --> 00:09:48.690
within the NHS as it currently stands.

00:09:48.690 --> 00:09:52.899
That is going to consume the vast amount of
the resource that we have available to us

00:09:52.899 --> 00:09:56.079
in 2016/2017, and I will come back to that
theme.

00:09:56.079 --> 00:10:01.610
There is a small amount of additional transformational
capacity that we will have in 2016/2017, but

00:10:01.610 --> 00:10:06.470
really it starts to kick in in 2017/2018 onwards,
and it builds through the period.

00:10:06.470 --> 00:10:12.440
Those twin goals: both there from the start,
but they begin with a focus on the re-stabilisation

00:10:12.440 --> 00:10:18.180
and then gradually we can do rather more on
the pace of the transformation.

00:10:18.180 --> 00:10:22.829
We will use the clarity which this particular
settlement has given us to give what the system

00:10:22.829 --> 00:10:28.620
has been asking of us repeatedly, which is
visibility and certainty around the resources

00:10:28.620 --> 00:10:32.600
that will be available to plan with and then
execute those strategies.

00:10:32.600 --> 00:10:33.990
That is what we will do.

00:10:33.990 --> 00:10:38.959
We will set out three-year allocations which
are binding, and two-year allocations which

00:10:38.959 --> 00:10:41.220
are more indicative in character.

00:10:41.220 --> 00:10:44.970
As always, there are terms and conditions
that apply; they are set out later in the

00:10:44.970 --> 00:10:46.480
paper in one of the annexes.

00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:50.600
Clearly, if unexpected events take place,
we will have to revisit those things, but

00:10:50.600 --> 00:10:56.519
it is our firm and serious intent that we
are setting out clarity for the system for

00:10:56.519 --> 00:11:01.350
three years firmly, two years indicatively,
which they can then act upon.

00:11:01.350 --> 00:11:07.410
That puts a big onus on us, of course, today,
to have the foresight to know how to allocate

00:11:07.410 --> 00:11:11.450
that money across the various priorities that
we have, because if we are doing it for that

00:11:11.450 --> 00:11:15.520
period of time it has to be robust.

00:11:15.520 --> 00:11:19.040
Can I just check before I go into the detailed
proposals – it is not one of the questions,

00:11:19.040 --> 00:11:24.800
perhaps, that will be posed in the paper – is
the Board comfortable, content, to commit

00:11:24.800 --> 00:11:28.639
funds in the way that I have just set out,
before we come to how we commit to them, to

00:11:28.639 --> 00:11:31.370
ensure that we have the clarity going forward?

00:11:31.370 --> 00:11:38.350
I think, Paul, there are just some important
caveats around things that may change, which

00:11:38.350 --> 00:11:40.459
give us the opt-out in case things fundamentally
go differently.

00:11:40.459 --> 00:11:42.050
We need to recognise that.

00:11:42.050 --> 00:11:43.050
Of course, absolutely.

00:11:43.050 --> 00:11:47.460
I think they are set out in the first of the
annexes in the paper, if people want to look

00:11:47.460 --> 00:11:52.440
at them, but yes, we have clearly had to set
out a variety of circumstances in which we

00:11:52.440 --> 00:11:55.329
would have to re-open elements of the settlement.

00:11:55.329 --> 00:12:00.339
Let us move on to the first of three decisions,
then.

00:12:00.339 --> 00:12:04.850
The three decisions, I would remind you, are
the same as the ones we had last year and

00:12:04.850 --> 00:12:05.850
the year before.

00:12:05.850 --> 00:12:09.770
Firstly, how do we want to distribute the
money between the various commissioning streams

00:12:09.770 --> 00:12:11.890
for which we are accountable?

00:12:11.890 --> 00:12:16.050
How much goes to CCGs, how much goes to primary
care, and so on.

00:12:16.050 --> 00:12:20.390
The second question that we have to address
is: what is the method by which we target

00:12:20.390 --> 00:12:25.800
the money within each of those streams to
best reflect the need, the met need and the

00:12:25.800 --> 00:12:28.980
unmet need, which results in those streams?

00:12:28.980 --> 00:12:32.699
The third question, having established the
target funding for those various commissioning

00:12:32.699 --> 00:12:38.889
streams by organisation: how quickly should
we move from where we currently are in allocation

00:12:38.889 --> 00:12:43.690
terms, to the allocations that we have set
out as the targets?

00:12:43.690 --> 00:12:47.610
Those are the three discrete decisions, and
I will walk us through each of those decisions

00:12:47.610 --> 00:12:49.200
in turn.

00:12:49.200 --> 00:12:54.480
Beginning first, then, with the all-important,
I think – this time as always – distribution

00:12:54.480 --> 00:12:56.839
of money between the commissioning streams.

00:12:56.839 --> 00:13:03.570
I have rehearsed in paragraph six the principles
which are largely consistent with what we

00:13:03.570 --> 00:13:07.910
have done in prior years, but all the more
important this year, I think.

00:13:07.910 --> 00:13:12.209
Firstly, as Simon has set as one of his tests
for the Spending Review, so we need to set

00:13:12.209 --> 00:13:17.800
for ourselves in these allocations, the reasonableness
of the efficiency challenge that we are setting

00:13:17.800 --> 00:13:19.200
for each of the streams.

00:13:19.200 --> 00:13:23.470
Clearly, we are going to have to stretch to
deliver the sorts of efficiencies we committed

00:13:23.470 --> 00:13:28.440
to delivering in the Forward View, but it
is important that we have equal ambition on

00:13:28.440 --> 00:13:31.380
each of the streams for which we are accountable.

00:13:31.380 --> 00:13:36.420
Secondly, we have an existing instrument of
policy, strengthened through the Forward View,

00:13:36.420 --> 00:13:41.050
of ensuring that primary care – and in particular,
the medical part of the primary care, but

00:13:41.050 --> 00:13:45.980
indeed, more broadly primary care – receives
a strong amount of additional resource to

00:13:45.980 --> 00:13:48.800
do the job that it needs to do in the Forward
View.

00:13:48.800 --> 00:13:53.480
Thirdly, as part of the way to create the
capacity to put more money to the front line

00:13:53.480 --> 00:14:00.170
and to have the funds to resource our transformation,
we have had to bear down heavily on the central

00:14:00.170 --> 00:14:04.750
budget which NHS England itself administers,
and you will see the evidence of that in the

00:14:04.750 --> 00:14:08.030
flow of money that we have.

00:14:08.030 --> 00:14:13.050
We are reconfirming the commitment that we
made last year to parity of esteem; in other

00:14:13.050 --> 00:14:18.160
words, ensuring that the investment that goes
into mental health is as strong as the investment

00:14:18.160 --> 00:14:20.620
that goes into all other aspects of health.

00:14:20.620 --> 00:14:26.610
The sorts of rules – or indeed, the specific
rule – that we put in place for 2015/2016,

00:14:26.610 --> 00:14:31.269
whereby investment in mental health by each
CCG and the system as a whole needs to be

00:14:31.269 --> 00:14:37.069
at least growing at the pace of the rest of
the allocations, we will maintain as we take

00:14:37.069 --> 00:14:39.709
this forward through this longer period.

00:14:39.709 --> 00:14:45.860
Secondly, the commitment to the Better Care
Fund will continue in the current form.

00:14:45.860 --> 00:14:50.300
More broadly then, over the next paragraphs,
I have set out the pressures that are faced

00:14:50.300 --> 00:14:53.440
by each of the commissioning streams, because
clearly the allocation to each of them needs

00:14:53.440 --> 00:14:58.160
to cover – as does the Spending Review itself
– the pressures that we face in each year

00:14:58.160 --> 00:14:59.490
in each commissioning stream.

00:14:59.490 --> 00:15:04.089
We have derived these directly from the modelling
that was done for the Spending Review itself,

00:15:04.089 --> 00:15:07.980
and have essentially just parcelled up the
Spending Review assumptions into the commissioning

00:15:07.980 --> 00:15:09.029
streams.

00:15:09.029 --> 00:15:12.930
Simon has already mentioned it, but essentially,
in looking at the £3.8 billion that we have

00:15:12.930 --> 00:15:18.829
in 2016/2017, it is important to remember
that we enter the year with a provider sector

00:15:18.829 --> 00:15:23.290
deficit of something in the order of £1.8
billion.

00:15:23.290 --> 00:15:26.949
There are many who would say that might end
up being a little bit higher than that, but

00:15:26.949 --> 00:15:30.470
the assumption in the Spending Review at least
is that we have a £1.8 billion deficit.

00:15:30.470 --> 00:15:34.180
That needs to be funded and has been funded
within the £3.8 billion; I will come back

00:15:34.180 --> 00:15:37.880
to the way in which that will be done.

00:15:37.880 --> 00:15:45.759
Secondly, we have about £1 billion of new
pensions pressure in 2016/2017, which needs

00:15:45.759 --> 00:15:48.060
to be funded within each of the commissioning
streams.

00:15:48.060 --> 00:15:52.629
So if you start from £3.8 billion, assume
that the £2.8 billion that I have just described

00:15:52.629 --> 00:15:59.149
is necessary, and you can see what we have
left after that.

00:15:59.149 --> 00:16:03.500
Pulling out just a few of the strands, I just
want to alight briefly on specialised commissioning.

00:16:03.500 --> 00:16:06.759
I think this is an important discussion for
the Board, here.

00:16:06.759 --> 00:16:09.709
In the modelling you will see in the table
which I will come to right at the end, you

00:16:09.709 --> 00:16:16.380
will see that we have allowed for 7% of growth
in allocations for specialised commissioning,

00:16:16.380 --> 00:16:21.819
and 4.5–5% of growth in specialised commissioning
after 2016/2017.

00:16:21.819 --> 00:16:27.259
The momentum level of pressure after all available
efficiencies in specialised commissioning

00:16:27.259 --> 00:16:30.290
is of the order of 4.5–5%.

00:16:30.290 --> 00:16:34.730
If you look back over history, if you look
now, that is broadly what it is.

00:16:34.730 --> 00:16:40.430
What we are facing, however, is an unprecedented
stream of major advances, to be welcomed in

00:16:40.430 --> 00:16:46.000
many ways, in highly effective drugs, highly
effective treatments in specialised commissioning.

00:16:46.000 --> 00:16:51.920
Unfortunately, those same effective treatments
have the unifying characteristic of being

00:16:51.920 --> 00:16:57.370
typically extremely expensive in terms of
the absolute amount of money that is required

00:16:57.370 --> 00:16:58.910
to bring them forward.

00:16:58.910 --> 00:17:05.060
So we have had to factor that in to the 2016/2017
estimate, which is why you have 7% rather

00:17:05.060 --> 00:17:12.990
than 4.5 5%, but I have to tell the Board:
that is an exceptionally low estimate of the

00:17:12.990 --> 00:17:18.130
pipeline that we are facing of these drugs
and treatments even in 2016/2017, and there

00:17:18.130 --> 00:17:23.209
is a substantial risk in setting it even at
7% that it will be significantly greater than

00:17:23.209 --> 00:17:24.230
that.

00:17:24.230 --> 00:17:29.570
Secondly, the assumption that we can then
live with 4.5–5% after that requires some

00:17:29.570 --> 00:17:34.420
belief about where that pipeline is going
to go in future years.

00:17:34.420 --> 00:17:39.169
The proposition I put in the paper is that
something is going to have to give.

00:17:39.169 --> 00:17:44.559
We are going to have to have some serious
discussion about the pace at which these particular

00:17:44.559 --> 00:17:45.559
treatments get adopted.

00:17:45.559 --> 00:17:50.840
We are going to have to have some very interesting
conversations, I think, with the industry

00:17:50.840 --> 00:17:54.539
about how that can be made affordable, and
we are going to have to think carefully about

00:17:54.539 --> 00:17:58.980
how things we do in other parts of the pathway
can reduce the amount of pressure on specialised

00:17:58.980 --> 00:18:00.100
commissioning.

00:18:00.100 --> 00:18:05.640
Because the only other way that I can do this
is to give more growth in 2016/2017 and even

00:18:05.640 --> 00:18:10.200
more growth in the subsequent years to specialised
commissioning, and the only three places that

00:18:10.200 --> 00:18:14.960
can obviously come from are CCG allocations,
which are needed to do all the things locally

00:18:14.960 --> 00:18:20.490
that we obviously have been discussing extensively
and we will discuss in the planning discussion;

00:18:20.490 --> 00:18:25.670
secondly, primary care, and that would go
directly contrary to the particular strategy

00:18:25.670 --> 00:18:31.590
that we have to ensure primary care is geared
up to deliver the transformation; or the transformation

00:18:31.590 --> 00:18:35.870
funds themselves, which I will come to, which
clearly would hold us back in taking all of

00:18:35.870 --> 00:18:37.120
those things forward.

00:18:37.120 --> 00:18:41.630
We have to find a way of making sure that
specialised commissioning can be contained

00:18:41.630 --> 00:18:45.360
within the sorts of numbers that we have in
this sheet.

00:18:45.360 --> 00:18:49.039
In the paper, it says, ‘Can we take them
down further in 2016/2017?’

00:18:49.039 --> 00:18:52.130
I think it is an interesting thing to put
in the paper; I can think of no strategy which

00:18:52.130 --> 00:18:55.490
would get it lower than what I have in the
paper for 2016/2017.

00:18:55.490 --> 00:19:00.720
We might want to come back to specialised
commissioning when we have finished this particular

00:19:00.720 --> 00:19:01.720
session.

00:19:01.720 --> 00:19:07.500
On page 5, we get into the transformational
and sustainability elements that we have allowed

00:19:07.500 --> 00:19:10.340
for in our allocations’ commissioning streams.

00:19:10.340 --> 00:19:19.850
We have £2.1 billion in 2016/2017, rising
to £3.4 billion in 2020/2021, shown in the

00:19:19.850 --> 00:19:20.850
numbers.

00:19:20.850 --> 00:19:25.730
That is made up of a transformation element
to cover seven-day services, GP access, cancer,

00:19:25.730 --> 00:19:28.010
mental health and prevention, and a variety
of other things.

00:19:28.010 --> 00:19:34.480
Then in 2016/2017 in particular, the sustainability
element of the fund, which is designed to

00:19:34.480 --> 00:19:41.350
deal with, once and for all, the provider
deficit that we have built to in 2015/2016,

00:19:41.350 --> 00:19:45.240
to bring the provider sector back into financial
balance.

00:19:45.240 --> 00:19:49.530
That will be discharged – I think the press
release, the announcement, was made yesterday

00:19:49.530 --> 00:19:55.720
– in two parts: a general element, which
will go to all providers of emergency services;

00:19:55.720 --> 00:19:59.600
and a targeted element, which will go to those
who can make additional efficiency gains.

00:19:59.600 --> 00:20:03.780
There will be some very strong elements of
conditionality around it, which is designed

00:20:03.780 --> 00:20:08.630
to ensure it is not just money that deals
with the deficit, but in so doing causes a

00:20:08.630 --> 00:20:13.100
focus on the things that are really important
for the provider sector as much as the commissioning

00:20:13.100 --> 00:20:19.150
sector to address: around setting the financial
position right, around delivering the core

00:20:19.150 --> 00:20:23.050
constitutional commitments, and around the
transformational work that we are trying to

00:20:23.050 --> 00:20:27.320
do; for example, through the integrated planning
processes, which will very much be a process

00:20:27.320 --> 00:20:30.970
for commissioners and providers to address
together.

00:20:30.970 --> 00:20:35.760
It is a fund to deal with a financial issue,
but it is a fund which also has to fulfil

00:20:35.760 --> 00:20:40.279
a strategic purpose in getting focus on those
things as well.

00:20:40.279 --> 00:20:44.270
Over the five-year period, the £1.8 billion
that we use next year for that Sustainability

00:20:44.270 --> 00:20:48.970
Fund will continue to be available to us,
and to the extent that we can get it to leverage

00:20:48.970 --> 00:20:53.640
big efficiency gains in the provider sector,
we will be able to liberate some of the £1.8

00:20:53.640 --> 00:20:58.640
billion to add to the other resources for
transformation that we have, to make up a

00:20:58.640 --> 00:21:03.300
bigger pot for us to speed up the pace of
transformation.

00:21:03.300 --> 00:21:07.660
That is the purpose that we have for it, and
we will also, as time goes by, explore how

00:21:07.660 --> 00:21:12.309
much of it needs to be retained and managed
on a national basis, and, as a matter of policy,

00:21:12.309 --> 00:21:18.990
how much we can get rapidly to the front line
in the form of devolved access to those funds.

00:21:18.990 --> 00:21:23.760
In the context of which, let me just mention
in slightly more detail the point which I

00:21:23.760 --> 00:21:28.490
think Simon alluded to in his introduction,
or rather his report, which is that, as part

00:21:28.490 --> 00:21:33.800
of the transformation funding, we have agreed
with Greater Manchester to carve out £450

00:21:33.800 --> 00:21:39.539
million – their fair share of those available
transformation budgets – for them to discharge

00:21:39.539 --> 00:21:45.250
in the context of their health and social
care strategic plan, where they are ahead

00:21:45.250 --> 00:21:48.720
of the rest of the country at the moment in
addressing the sort of integrated planning

00:21:48.720 --> 00:21:53.700
that we will come to in June or thereabouts
with the rest of the country.

00:21:53.700 --> 00:21:57.050
I would just invite the Board specifically
– it is not stated as one of the decisions

00:21:57.050 --> 00:22:00.429
that we are requiring of the Board in the
paper – but when we come to the end of this

00:22:00.429 --> 00:22:05.090
section which I am very nearly at, it would
be good if you could express your support

00:22:05.090 --> 00:22:09.730
or otherwise for that particular allocation
to Greater Manchester, which I was discussing

00:22:09.730 --> 00:22:15.120
with them yesterday but clearly was subject
to your approval today.

00:22:15.120 --> 00:22:22.710
It is just worth saying: I talked about efficiencies
earlier; clearly, we have built into it the

00:22:22.710 --> 00:22:28.429
2% provider efficiency, which has been previously
discussed as being the level that will be

00:22:28.429 --> 00:22:32.620
put in the tariff consultation to come out
shortly.

00:22:32.620 --> 00:22:36.480
We have also factored into here the activity-related
savings which, if you remember the Forward

00:22:36.480 --> 00:22:40.650
View, kick in progressively as we go forward
with our transformation.

00:22:40.650 --> 00:22:44.840
These have been built in as activity-related
savings, predominantly in the back-end of

00:22:44.840 --> 00:22:46.870
the period of modelling.

00:22:46.870 --> 00:22:51.110
There are two important caveats which reflect
back to Simon’s opening that, clearly, our

00:22:51.110 --> 00:22:56.040
ability to drive those activity-related savings
will to an extent be dependent on the level

00:22:56.040 --> 00:23:01.049
of public health investment behind prevention;
and to an extent, also the degree to which

00:23:01.049 --> 00:23:08.510
we are insulated from social care underfunding
aspects, should that not be resolved.

00:23:08.510 --> 00:23:12.720
Those are quite important caveats around the
activity-related savings.

00:23:12.720 --> 00:23:16.559
Clearly, you will recall what the activity-related
savings are derived from.

00:23:16.559 --> 00:23:19.929
The biggest single engine of that numerically
is the Right Care Programme, which we have

00:23:19.929 --> 00:23:24.460
discussed a number of times, and is our key
instrument of allocative efficiency, getting

00:23:24.460 --> 00:23:26.150
rid of waste and variation.

00:23:26.150 --> 00:23:31.789
However, the new models of care and self-care
are also required to make their contribution

00:23:31.789 --> 00:23:33.350
in the latter part of the period.

00:23:33.350 --> 00:23:39.380
If all of that comes together, which is our
expectation, you get Table 2, which describes

00:23:39.380 --> 00:23:42.760
the place-based commissioning budgets in the
first few lines; in other words, the things

00:23:42.760 --> 00:23:49.240
which represent local commissioning activity;
whether done by CCGs for themselves, whether

00:23:49.240 --> 00:23:54.520
done in delegated form or otherwise for primary
care medical services, or whether done through

00:23:54.520 --> 00:23:58.920
our hubs, but increasingly through co-commissioning
with CCGs, in the specialised space.

00:23:58.920 --> 00:24:03.440
You can see those budgets as they are billed
there; and in particular, the strong growth

00:24:03.440 --> 00:24:05.620
that we have allowed for in primary care.

00:24:05.620 --> 00:24:09.290
You can see the Sustainability and Transformation
Fund in the next line, which I have broadly

00:24:09.290 --> 00:24:10.789
discussed already.

00:24:10.789 --> 00:24:15.279
You can see other direct commissioning, which
are the small number of other services which

00:24:15.279 --> 00:24:17.840
we commission as NHS England.

00:24:17.840 --> 00:24:21.460
You can see the declining amount of NHS England
central budgets, which reflects the earlier

00:24:21.460 --> 00:24:23.210
comment that I made.

00:24:23.210 --> 00:24:28.559
Then the ongoing use of draw-down; in other
words, use of historic surpluses to deal with

00:24:28.559 --> 00:24:34.870
non-recurrent investments, and also to deal
with CCGs who are still in deficit.

00:24:34.870 --> 00:24:40.210
One hopes that that will be a declining number
as we go forward, as we correct the allocative

00:24:40.210 --> 00:24:44.990
efficiencies in the latter part of the proposals,
and secondly that that will be used as an

00:24:44.990 --> 00:24:49.440
additional kicker to the transformation that
we have been talking about.

00:24:49.440 --> 00:24:53.309
That is probably enough from me on allocation
to the commissioning streams.

00:24:53.309 --> 00:24:59.460
I am open now to either questions or a decision
of the Board as to whether these should be

00:24:59.460 --> 00:25:00.840
the allocations to the streams.

00:25:00.840 --> 00:25:01.840
Malcolm?

00:25:01.840 --> 00:25:03.080
Right, please let me invite questions.

00:25:03.080 --> 00:25:08.690
I will take the formal approval at the end
of the item, if I may, but I would like to

00:25:08.690 --> 00:25:20.340
take questions at this stage, or comments
on the introduction of the paper from Paul.

00:25:20.340 --> 00:25:24.490
A lot to like, obviously.

00:25:24.490 --> 00:25:27.690
No surprise; place-based stuff is really good.

00:25:27.690 --> 00:25:35.029
3+2 is far better than doing this every year,
consistent with what we have been saying.

00:25:35.029 --> 00:25:43.950
I suppose the specialised commissioning one:
you alluded to it Paul, but this has to be

00:25:43.950 --> 00:25:50.320
about reducing demand as much as it is also
about saying: ‘What are the more innovative

00:25:50.320 --> 00:25:52.669
ways of paying for this stuff?’

00:25:52.669 --> 00:25:57.799
Because unless it is both, I just cannot see
how the numbers work.

00:25:57.799 --> 00:26:04.610
The other side is, if we have 7% inflation,
or even 4.5% inflation year on year, we will

00:26:04.610 --> 00:26:09.070
never make the strategic shift, so this is
existential, I think.

00:26:09.070 --> 00:26:16.980
Maybe it is not for now, but we really need
to say what we are going to do to make that

00:26:16.980 --> 00:26:21.100
happen, so we do not get to 2017/2018 and
say, ‘Oh, magically it is supposed to be

00:26:21.100 --> 00:26:22.210
4.5%.’

00:26:22.210 --> 00:26:24.450
I agree.

00:26:24.450 --> 00:26:25.880
That feels quite major.

00:26:25.880 --> 00:26:31.770
There is a cultural thing in it as well, because
the example that always sticks with me is,

00:26:31.770 --> 00:26:38.990
in my previous life in colorectal cancer,
we know that recurrence falls if it is about

00:26:38.990 --> 00:26:39.990
exercise.

00:26:39.990 --> 00:26:44.730
So it is not only saying, ‘Can we be more
efficient in colorectal cancer surgery?’,

00:26:44.730 --> 00:26:49.299
but it is around saying, ‘Actually, we really
need to – not only for the benefit of the

00:26:49.299 --> 00:26:54.679
patient – convince primary care that exercise
is a good thing, and by the way it will have

00:26:54.679 --> 00:26:55.679
a knock-on effect.’

00:26:55.679 --> 00:26:57.549
So there is a huge cultural thing behind this,
as well.

00:26:57.549 --> 00:26:59.980
It is not just a technocratic thing here.

00:26:59.980 --> 00:27:00.980
I totally agree.

00:27:00.980 --> 00:27:05.600
It is kind of an endorsement, but it is an
existential question, I think.

00:27:05.600 --> 00:27:09.980
I agree with everything you said.

00:27:09.980 --> 00:27:14.100
I think there are three strands to that, one
of which is, I think we might want to do a

00:27:14.100 --> 00:27:17.520
bit more thought leadership around that as
part of the input to the integrated planning

00:27:17.520 --> 00:27:18.750
that is coming along.

00:27:18.750 --> 00:27:21.720
Secondly, in the integrated planning, it is
our intention that specialised commissioning

00:27:21.720 --> 00:27:25.919
will be very much part of what is done in
the relevant localities.

00:27:25.919 --> 00:27:29.630
The third thing is, in the Right Care Programme,
I would say that specialised commissioning

00:27:29.630 --> 00:27:32.630
has been a small part of it to date.

00:27:32.630 --> 00:27:36.320
As we roll out the Right Care Programme, it
would again be very important, I think, that

00:27:36.320 --> 00:27:41.279
we integrate insights into specialised commissioning
and potential opportunities there to shift

00:27:41.279 --> 00:27:44.660
the way in which the whole pathway is managed.

00:27:44.660 --> 00:27:48.350
That might give us another entrée into the
question that you raised.

00:27:48.350 --> 00:27:51.940
It is why population evidence and place-based
is so critical, because otherwise we are just

00:27:51.940 --> 00:28:02.740
building a better sailing ship, and actually
we need something which has a bit more steam

00:28:02.740 --> 00:28:05.520
power or something.

00:28:05.520 --> 00:28:09.019
Paul, thank you very much.

00:28:09.019 --> 00:28:19.020
Can you just clarify for me: the £1.8 billion
fund that is allocated to deal with the provider

00:28:19.020 --> 00:28:33.019
deficit is predicated on our realising a 2%
efficiency, and if we are counting now as

00:28:33.019 --> 00:28:38.300
year zero, we have already realised 2% efficiency.

00:28:38.300 --> 00:28:47.850
What is the link if we, at the end of this
year, do not quite realise that 2%?

00:28:47.850 --> 00:28:53.309
What is the link between that and the £1.8
billion?

00:28:53.309 --> 00:28:55.360
I think it is very clear.

00:28:55.360 --> 00:29:00.220
The £1.8 billion is all we have to solve
the provider challenge next year.

00:29:00.220 --> 00:29:05.649
If we do not enter the year with a £1.8 billion
deficit, if it is higher than that, what is

00:29:05.649 --> 00:29:09.880
going to have to happen is that, in setting
the control totals that NHS Improvement will

00:29:09.880 --> 00:29:14.210
do for each of the individual organisations
around the country as part of their entry

00:29:14.210 --> 00:29:18.980
to the scheme, so to speak, additional efficiency
will have to be built in over and above the

00:29:18.980 --> 00:29:24.409
2% on an individual basis to get down to the
level at which £1.8 billion does eradicate

00:29:24.409 --> 00:29:25.840
the provider deficit.

00:29:25.840 --> 00:29:30.850
It is really, really important that this year,
every effort is made to get down to that level

00:29:30.850 --> 00:29:36.640
already, because that will be the best way
to ensure that next year the 2% is what is

00:29:36.640 --> 00:29:37.700
needed.

00:29:37.700 --> 00:29:43.580
However, as you rightly point out, if that
is not done it just ups the requirement next

00:29:43.580 --> 00:29:47.510
year of individual providers, and particularly
individual providers who have not managed

00:29:47.510 --> 00:29:51.890
to hold their expenditure this year to the
level implied by that.

00:29:51.890 --> 00:29:55.779
One of the other things I should have made
clear perhaps, because I think it is easy

00:29:55.779 --> 00:30:00.360
to misunderstand the 2% as being a massive
reduction, as it were: 2% needs to be real

00:30:00.360 --> 00:30:04.530
efficiency; real, delivered productivity on
the ground.

00:30:04.530 --> 00:30:09.130
Not notional efficiencies, not hypothetical
efficiencies and all that sort of stuff.

00:30:09.130 --> 00:30:11.260
There is a hard edge to the 2%.

00:30:11.260 --> 00:30:17.580
It is challenging enough, certainly given
the recent trends in underlying productivity,

00:30:17.580 --> 00:30:21.370
and it has to be 2% that really does take
cost out of the system.

00:30:21.370 --> 00:30:27.409
It might be worth, Paul, just adding that
the £1.8 billion and the 2% cannot be seen

00:30:27.409 --> 00:30:32.620
in isolation from the approach we are taking
on tariff, which will be subject to consultation.

00:30:32.620 --> 00:30:37.169
So there is also a 3.1% cash uplift built
into tariff.

00:30:37.169 --> 00:30:43.080
The sequencing of this is that providers saw
that we were going to be proposing the 3.1%

00:30:43.080 --> 00:30:48.659
cash uplift and a more modest 2% efficiency,
i.e., at least a 1% rise in prices, and thought,

00:30:48.659 --> 00:30:53.170
‘Okay, that sort of sounds constructive
but does not go the whole way,’ and then

00:30:53.170 --> 00:30:56.910
what we are doing yesterday and today is putting
another £1.8 billion on the table.

00:30:56.910 --> 00:31:02.840
It is those pieces combined that I think mean
that the provider sector rightly believes,

00:31:02.840 --> 00:31:08.769
certainly as articulated by NHS Improvement
and Jim Mackie, that this is a feasible and

00:31:08.769 --> 00:31:10.970
doable settlement for next year.

00:31:10.970 --> 00:31:16.960
Actually, I think a lot of my question has
just been answered really, which was about

00:31:16.960 --> 00:31:20.730
the exit positioning and, if that was worse,
how we would handle it.

00:31:20.730 --> 00:31:26.639
I think if I can maybe move on to the Transformation
and Sustainability Fund.

00:31:26.639 --> 00:31:35.799
Clearly, it is absolutely critical that the
Sustainability Fund has real teeth in terms

00:31:35.799 --> 00:31:42.019
of ensuring ongoing improvements and efficiency,
otherwise we do not have the degree of transformation

00:31:42.019 --> 00:31:44.760
funding that we will need in subsequent years.

00:31:44.760 --> 00:31:50.570
I guess my question, Paul, is: to what extent
do you think – and how can you reassure

00:31:50.570 --> 00:31:58.289
the Board – that there is sufficient conditionality
on that sustainability funding that will give

00:31:58.289 --> 00:32:03.669
us confidence that the deficit positions in
due course will get better not worse; and

00:32:03.669 --> 00:32:12.830
also, that those who have managed their finances
the best will be in a position to be recognised

00:32:12.830 --> 00:32:16.410
for that?

00:32:16.410 --> 00:32:18.210
First question first, so the conditionality.

00:32:18.210 --> 00:32:20.039
I have broadly alluded to it in the paper.

00:32:20.039 --> 00:32:25.639
I think a lot of it was set out in the announcements
yesterday, but there will be three areas of

00:32:25.639 --> 00:32:30.610
conditionality, in broad terms, with sub-elements
of that.

00:32:30.610 --> 00:32:34.980
The first being the fundamental financial
one; so to your earlier point, if organisations

00:32:34.980 --> 00:32:38.690
do not hit the control totals that have been
set or do not achieve the efficiencies which

00:32:38.690 --> 00:32:45.260
are set out as part of that control total,
in particular around implementing the agency

00:32:45.260 --> 00:32:49.639
measures that have been taken this year and
will continue into next year – in particular,

00:32:49.639 --> 00:32:56.480
in terms of implementing the work following
Lord Carter’s study on efficiency – clearly

00:32:56.480 --> 00:33:01.690
that conditionality will bite, and it will
be very specific in terms of conditionality

00:33:01.690 --> 00:33:02.690
around that.

00:33:02.690 --> 00:33:06.190
The constitutional standards I have mentioned
already, and they will be the ones that you

00:33:06.190 --> 00:33:07.190
would expect.

00:33:07.190 --> 00:33:12.669
Then thirdly, in some ways, I would not say
the most important but almost equally important,

00:33:12.669 --> 00:33:14.909
is the engagement with the transformational
agenda.

00:33:14.909 --> 00:33:19.960
For me in particular, that centres around
the quality of engagement and the quality

00:33:19.960 --> 00:33:26.070
of the content of the integrated plans that
every provider will be getting together with

00:33:26.070 --> 00:33:28.350
its commissioners to produce in the summer.

00:33:28.350 --> 00:33:32.740
Again, I will come later to conditionality
around the commissioner engagement with that,

00:33:32.740 --> 00:33:37.510
but there is a direct mirror image of that
in the conditionality around the sustainability

00:33:37.510 --> 00:33:40.990
element of the fund.

00:33:40.990 --> 00:33:44.409
Paul, may we turn to specialised commissioning?

00:33:44.409 --> 00:33:52.130
Because you outlined to us what the implications
are if we did not cap it at a 7% growth rate,

00:33:52.130 --> 00:33:55.160
and the implications for taking funding from
other spheres.

00:33:55.160 --> 00:34:01.100
I think what the Board needs to know as well
is: what assurance can you give us as to the

00:34:01.100 --> 00:34:07.460
realistic character of that 7% cap, and in
particular in relation to high-cost drugs

00:34:07.460 --> 00:34:13.169
and medical devices and the change in character
of new drugs that are coming through; and

00:34:13.169 --> 00:34:18.430
also, whether you are confident that this
is a realisable number?

00:34:18.430 --> 00:34:24.680
First thing to say is, I would not put it
in the allocations if I did not think it was

00:34:24.680 --> 00:34:25.750
a realisable number.

00:34:25.750 --> 00:34:29.930
It is not a balancing figure that I have generated
to make the numbers balance.

00:34:29.930 --> 00:34:34.180
It is the one that worries me most of all
the numbers in the 2016/2017 position, for

00:34:34.180 --> 00:34:35.970
the reasons that I have set out.

00:34:35.970 --> 00:34:40.210
To an extent, it worries me because there
is, as things currently stand, not a great

00:34:40.210 --> 00:34:44.220
deal that we can do to manage the flow of
these things.

00:34:44.220 --> 00:34:49.680
I think that is the bit that we need to change
going in to 2016/2017, because if it is outside

00:34:49.680 --> 00:34:53.890
our control and we have a budget to manage,
it is really quite tricky.

00:34:53.890 --> 00:34:57.750
It is the biggest area of risk; I think I
have made that clear to the Board as I went

00:34:57.750 --> 00:34:58.750
through.

00:34:58.750 --> 00:35:03.250
It is a realisable figure, but it is a figure
which carries a substantial amount of risk

00:35:03.250 --> 00:35:07.020
attached to it, and we have seen in recent
years the different reasons for specialised

00:35:07.020 --> 00:35:08.820
commissioning budget overspend.

00:35:08.820 --> 00:35:13.780
We believe that we have the Cancer Drugs Fund
through the consultation that is currently

00:35:13.780 --> 00:35:17.690
underway to a point where we can manage that
in an appropriate fashion.

00:35:17.690 --> 00:35:24.010
You have seen in the results for this year
that the grip on the operational performance

00:35:24.010 --> 00:35:27.590
of specialised commissioning in its normal
– if there is such a thing in specialised

00:35:27.590 --> 00:35:31.920
commissioning – its routine, regional commissioning,
you have seen that come under significantly

00:35:31.920 --> 00:35:35.100
better control and grip than it had before.

00:35:35.100 --> 00:35:38.680
The element that is most challenging is this
flow of drugs coming in, and there I think

00:35:38.680 --> 00:35:45.830
we will need to take assertive action to hold
it within the budget.

00:35:45.830 --> 00:35:50.820
I think on that, should the Board approve
this, then this is something that we will

00:35:50.820 --> 00:35:54.630
want to track very carefully over the coming
year and beyond.

00:35:54.630 --> 00:35:59.990
Let me invite any further questions for Paul
on this part of the paper before we move to

00:35:59.990 --> 00:36:01.480
the next stage.

00:36:01.480 --> 00:36:02.480
No.

00:36:02.480 --> 00:36:09.480
Turning to page 7 then, we come to the middle
bit of the three, which is the setting of

00:36:09.480 --> 00:36:13.600
the target allocations for each of the commissioning
streams.

00:36:13.600 --> 00:36:19.400
A really important piece of work which Simon
alluded to is the work on inequalities.

00:36:19.400 --> 00:36:23.750
We made big progress on that in the formulae
that we talked about last year, and indeed

00:36:23.750 --> 00:36:24.750
the year before.

00:36:24.750 --> 00:36:28.860
We have done more work on it this year, partly
in the search for the holy grail as to whether

00:36:28.860 --> 00:36:32.920
there is a much better way of doing the inequalities
adjustment, and I have to disappoint the Board

00:36:32.920 --> 00:36:38.200
by saying that despite a substantial amount
of research and looking around the world at

00:36:38.200 --> 00:36:41.740
other ways of doing it, we have not come up
with anything better than we had before.

00:36:41.740 --> 00:36:45.890
We looked with great interest at some work
which is alluded to in the paper by Ben Barr

00:36:45.890 --> 00:36:51.720
and colleagues, which indicated some kind
of marginal impact of greater investment in

00:36:51.720 --> 00:36:53.270
deprived areas than others.

00:36:53.270 --> 00:36:57.500
It did not lead us to anything – by his
agreement, I have to say – that was actionable

00:36:57.500 --> 00:37:01.480
in the form of a different form of inequalities
adjustment, so we have what we have.

00:37:01.480 --> 00:37:05.250
We have extended the inequalities adjustments
necessarily to include one for specialised

00:37:05.250 --> 00:37:09.160
commissioning, where we are introducing a
formula, as I will come to in a minute, for

00:37:09.160 --> 00:37:10.160
the first time.

00:37:10.160 --> 00:37:15.050
We have also made – again, with ACRA’s
advice on this one – a refinement to the

00:37:15.050 --> 00:37:20.700
way in which we measure the inequalities which
we then attach an adjustment in the formula

00:37:20.700 --> 00:37:25.700
to, to make it more sensitive to the highest
end of deprivation as we measure it through

00:37:25.700 --> 00:37:27.400
the metrics that we use here.

00:37:27.400 --> 00:37:32.140
So we have gone from a ten-tier system to
a 16-tier system, the top six tiers being,

00:37:32.140 --> 00:37:36.980
if you like, a more granular way of looking
at the most deprived economies, and that gives

00:37:36.980 --> 00:37:41.650
us a better fix, I think, on the position;
it will improve the sensitivity of the formula

00:37:41.650 --> 00:37:42.750
in that particular way.

00:37:42.750 --> 00:37:44.380
We have built that in on inequalities.

00:37:44.380 --> 00:37:47.810
I will come back to that if people wish to
discuss it.

00:37:47.810 --> 00:37:52.050
Secondly, we have obviously updated, as you
would expect, for population in general for

00:37:52.050 --> 00:37:53.460
all of the formulae.

00:37:53.460 --> 00:37:58.490
All of this is done on registered patient
lists, etc., and therefore the appropriateness

00:37:58.490 --> 00:38:02.750
of those patient lists is important, and work
has gone on to ensure that that remains the

00:38:02.750 --> 00:38:03.810
case.

00:38:03.810 --> 00:38:10.020
On the CCG formula, we have essentially updated
everything that moves in the CCG formula by

00:38:10.020 --> 00:38:14.360
something like four years in general from
when we first set the formulae, to the best

00:38:14.360 --> 00:38:16.380
information that is available now.

00:38:16.380 --> 00:38:22.340
We have made some minor changes to the emergency
ambulance cost adjustment, which you do not

00:38:22.340 --> 00:38:25.620
need to detain yourselves on because it has
a very small weighting within the overall

00:38:25.620 --> 00:38:26.620
formula.

00:38:26.620 --> 00:38:31.900
The much more important part of the changes
we have made to the CCG formula, at least

00:38:31.900 --> 00:38:36.190
for some parts of the country, is the introduction
of a sparsity adjustment; there is a whole

00:38:36.190 --> 00:38:40.520
annexe which sets out the work that we have
done on that, but the end consequence of that

00:38:40.520 --> 00:38:45.990
is that six CCGs in relation to eight remote
sites around the country will have an additional

00:38:45.990 --> 00:38:52.180
element to their allocations which reflect
our best judgement on the necessary additional

00:38:52.180 --> 00:38:57.600
costs which are faced by people who, because
of their geography and catchment area, have

00:38:57.600 --> 00:39:02.940
to operate at less than economic scale, so
we have introduced that to it.

00:39:02.940 --> 00:39:07.070
You can see the net effect of all of those
things, and I should say that all of those

00:39:07.070 --> 00:39:12.780
changes have been agreed in detail with ACRA,
which is as you know the independent body

00:39:12.780 --> 00:39:16.530
that kind of advises us on all of this stuff.

00:39:16.530 --> 00:39:21.240
The net effect of that, you can see set out
in terms of the distance from target in the

00:39:21.240 --> 00:39:23.630
table at paragraph 32.

00:39:23.630 --> 00:39:27.830
It does slightly increase our challenge because
it generates a few more underfunded CCGs,

00:39:27.830 --> 00:39:31.390
the combination of all those changes; particularly
in areas where there has been rapid growth,

00:39:31.390 --> 00:39:33.000
I think, in those CCGs.

00:39:33.000 --> 00:39:41.240
However, broadly speaking it does not fundamentally
shift the pattern of the target allocations,

00:39:41.240 --> 00:39:48.100
but it gets us to a much more robust and defensible
point of reflecting the need in those allocations.

00:39:48.100 --> 00:39:51.940
That is essentially what we have done on CCG
formula.

00:39:51.940 --> 00:39:56.550
There is a fundamental revision to the primary
care medical formula that we have.

00:39:56.550 --> 00:40:01.370
We have moved on from the Carr–Hill Formula
basis that we have had in previous primary

00:40:01.370 --> 00:40:06.080
care formulae, which were 15 years old, which
feels a little bit long in the tooth for something

00:40:06.080 --> 00:40:07.710
we are doing current allocations on.

00:40:07.710 --> 00:40:12.570
We have moved to a new basis of doing that
based on stratified workload of GPs in dealing

00:40:12.570 --> 00:40:17.660
with particular demographic types of patient,
if I can put it that way, and in so doing,

00:40:17.660 --> 00:40:26.040
then updated by those 15 years or so the accuracy
and meaningfulness of the need estimation

00:40:26.040 --> 00:40:27.110
that comes from that.

00:40:27.110 --> 00:40:30.020
Again, ACRA has endorsed those changes.

00:40:30.020 --> 00:40:33.640
Important to say that we are not at this stage
changing the formula by which we pay GPs;

00:40:33.640 --> 00:40:39.120
that is a wholly separate stream of development,
but at some point we will need to bring the

00:40:39.120 --> 00:40:41.290
two things a bit closer together over time.

00:40:41.290 --> 00:40:46.650
We are not changing the other elements of
primary care; the dentistry, the optical services

00:40:46.650 --> 00:40:48.490
and community pharmacy at this stage.

00:40:48.490 --> 00:40:52.710
Also, we are not – I should say, because
I might forget otherwise – we are not including

00:40:52.710 --> 00:40:57.790
that in the place-based calculations later
on, because they are typically not ones that

00:40:57.790 --> 00:41:03.090
we are doing through co-commissioning, etc.,
so they remain a central activity to a large

00:41:03.090 --> 00:41:04.160
extent.

00:41:04.160 --> 00:41:06.560
That is the change to the primary care formula.

00:41:06.560 --> 00:41:10.570
Again, for those who like these things, there
is an annexe with all the technicalities in

00:41:10.570 --> 00:41:13.550
it available for people.

00:41:13.550 --> 00:41:18.890
Then the real excitement of the year is the
introduction of a specialised formula, clearly

00:41:18.890 --> 00:41:23.100
important if we are going to do specialised
now increasingly on a co-commissioned basis

00:41:23.100 --> 00:41:25.150
as part of place-based allocations.

00:41:25.150 --> 00:41:31.050
We have based the workings of that formula
on the methodology we used for the CCG formula,

00:41:31.050 --> 00:41:35.700
but clearly the dynamics of specialised are
different.

00:41:35.700 --> 00:41:39.660
You can see that set out; I am now moving
into page 10.

00:41:39.660 --> 00:41:43.300
There is a particular challenge in specialised
because, as you will be aware from all sorts

00:41:43.300 --> 00:41:47.550
of other discussions we have had, the data
for many elements of specialised is more difficult

00:41:47.550 --> 00:41:52.140
to come by than it is for routine CCG commissioning,
if I can put it that way.

00:41:52.140 --> 00:41:57.040
There is about 50% of the specialised portfolio
where we have a strong enough set of data

00:41:57.040 --> 00:42:02.470
to be able to build a formula around it, and
for those 50%, we have constructed said formula

00:42:02.470 --> 00:42:03.960
and deployed it.

00:42:03.960 --> 00:42:09.180
For the other 50%, rather than making up numbers
or extrapolating in some strange way, which

00:42:09.180 --> 00:42:12.680
would not have worked because specialised
is characterised as being different in its

00:42:12.680 --> 00:42:18.120
various components rather than homogenous,
we are using existing baseline spend.

00:42:18.120 --> 00:42:23.950
So effectively, there is no stretch between
current and future spending in those parts.

00:42:23.950 --> 00:42:29.590
Clearly, as our data and as our cost capture
for specialised improves over time, we will

00:42:29.590 --> 00:42:35.340
be able to build a more comprehensive needs-based
formula, but for now we have only built in

00:42:35.340 --> 00:42:40.580
needs basis where we have robust data to do
that with.

00:42:40.580 --> 00:42:42.230
That is the specialised formula.

00:42:42.230 --> 00:42:46.840
I need to draw to your attention that this
has been done during the course of the year,

00:42:46.840 --> 00:42:50.650
has taken quite a lot of effort, and as a
consequence of that it does not yet have the

00:42:50.650 --> 00:42:51.880
ACRA stamp of approval.

00:42:51.880 --> 00:42:56.230
We have had positive discussions with ACRA
around the formula, but it is important just

00:42:56.230 --> 00:43:00.980
to state that they have not yet completed
the work they would need to do to say it is

00:43:00.980 --> 00:43:04.380
entirely what they can endorse.

00:43:04.380 --> 00:43:08.920
We will complete that work, to state the obvious,
I hope, and we will complete that over the

00:43:08.920 --> 00:43:15.360
next few months, but we are inviting the Board
to proceed with putting in specialised formulae

00:43:15.360 --> 00:43:17.730
on the basis that we have set out.

00:43:17.730 --> 00:43:22.100
Should ACRA come back with anything substantially
problematic in that respect, we will clearly

00:43:22.100 --> 00:43:26.300
then need to decide whether we restate or
do some adjustments for that.

00:43:26.300 --> 00:43:30.650
It is my expectation that ACRA will be able
to support it, but clearly that is a matter

00:43:30.650 --> 00:43:35.240
for them when they have a chance to run the
slide rule over our formula.

00:43:35.240 --> 00:43:39.090
I will come back later to the arguments as
to why I think it is really important that

00:43:39.090 --> 00:43:44.210
we do build specialised commissioning into
the pace of change, into the place based allocations,

00:43:44.210 --> 00:43:48.550
but you cannot do that without a formula,
and I am inviting you to adopt this particular

00:43:48.550 --> 00:43:49.550
formula.

00:43:49.550 --> 00:43:54.490
Finally, in this section on formulae and target
allocations, it is just important to say that

00:43:54.490 --> 00:43:58.780
all of this has been through a variety of
quality assurance processes, as you would

00:43:58.780 --> 00:44:01.140
expect that we do each year.

00:44:01.140 --> 00:44:05.411
They are set out there in their various components,
so it has been done to death, I think would

00:44:05.411 --> 00:44:06.451
be the best way to describe it.

00:44:06.451 --> 00:44:12.490
It has been done at pace, however, and as
a consequence of that, I am suggesting that,

00:44:12.490 --> 00:44:20.060
having taken whatever decisions we do today,
we will take the next few weeks just to double-check

00:44:20.060 --> 00:44:25.640
the specific allocations to individual organisations,
to make sure that no glitches have crept in.

00:44:25.640 --> 00:44:29.620
At the moment, all of that appears to be very
positive, and as I mentioned this morning,

00:44:29.620 --> 00:44:32.670
we have had a number of independent people
looking at that and finding it plausible.

00:44:32.670 --> 00:44:36.430
I would just like, given the complexity of
what we are about to come to, to have the

00:44:36.430 --> 00:44:40.780
opportunity to personally and with colleagues
just review the final set of numbers that

00:44:40.780 --> 00:44:45.860
emerge, the consequence of which would be
that we would publish the numbers for CCGs

00:44:45.860 --> 00:44:51.440
and the other components of place-based allocations
in the first week of January rather than rushing

00:44:51.440 --> 00:44:53.030
them out before Christmas.

00:44:53.030 --> 00:44:56.880
I think it is also important they are going
to need quite a bit of communication and explanation,

00:44:56.880 --> 00:45:01.090
because it is more complex, and I would rather
do that when people were focused on coming

00:45:01.090 --> 00:45:06.730
back for work rather than focused on going
off for turkeys and trimmings.

00:45:06.730 --> 00:45:09.270
That is my proposal, at least, in terms of
the handling of that.

00:45:09.270 --> 00:45:13.230
That is all I wanted to say about the middle
piece of this triptych.

00:45:13.230 --> 00:45:16.390
If there are any questions on the formulae,
that would be fine.

00:45:16.390 --> 00:45:19.240
Thank you, Paul.

00:45:19.240 --> 00:45:23.040
Let me invite questions.

00:45:23.040 --> 00:45:29.500
Paul, as you know from the Specialised Commissioning
Committee, John, Margaret and I receive frequent

00:45:29.500 --> 00:45:36.470
representations from different patient groups
who are concerned about the pace with which

00:45:36.470 --> 00:45:39.250
we implement co-commissioning of specialised.

00:45:39.250 --> 00:45:46.840
Am I right in thinking that the revision to
the specialist formula does not in itself

00:45:46.840 --> 00:45:52.670
accelerate co commissioning of specialised;
it provides a facility to do that should we

00:45:52.670 --> 00:46:03.900
wish, but its equal objective is to provide
a better allocative efficiency of specialised

00:46:03.900 --> 00:46:06.420
even if we continued to do it on a more centralised
basis?

00:46:06.420 --> 00:46:12.660
There is a one-word answer to that, which
is: yes, you are right; sorry, that is four

00:46:12.660 --> 00:46:13.660
words.

00:46:13.660 --> 00:46:18.970
Yes, you are right; the allocation method
has no impact whatsoever on who does what

00:46:18.970 --> 00:46:19.970
job.

00:46:19.970 --> 00:46:24.062
We are very keen, however, for reasons which
will become apparent when we come to the next

00:46:24.062 --> 00:46:29.050
section, to encourage the maximum possible
engagement of local economies with specialised

00:46:29.050 --> 00:46:31.090
commissioning, but that does imply that there
is a change.

00:46:31.090 --> 00:46:33.870
But that decision is taken ex the formula?

00:46:33.870 --> 00:46:34.870
Exactly right.

00:46:34.870 --> 00:46:39.400
As you know, Noel, what it does do is exemplify
the fact that there is no free lunch.

00:46:39.400 --> 00:46:44.060
So for the people of Greater Manchester or
the people of Cumbria or the people of Lewisham,

00:46:44.060 --> 00:46:50.040
if we are putting x-hundred million more into
hepatitis C treatments next year, then that

00:46:50.040 --> 00:46:53.470
x-hundred million is not available for primary
care and mental health, and at the moment,

00:46:53.470 --> 00:46:59.060
that has been invisible to the naked eye locally;
now, this makes it much more transparent.

00:46:59.060 --> 00:47:08.590
I do not detect any other questions coming
forward, Paul, so can I invite you to continue?

00:47:08.590 --> 00:47:15.980
Fasten your seatbelts, if you would, for the
challenging part of all of this, and frankly,

00:47:15.980 --> 00:47:21.960
for the next ten minutes, the least strategically
enwrapped part of the discussion.

00:47:21.960 --> 00:47:24.800
Bear with me please, because we do need to
get this right, because whilst the strategic

00:47:24.800 --> 00:47:29.510
stuff is incredibly important, if we mess
up the technical stuff it will all be entirely

00:47:29.510 --> 00:47:32.330
invalid, so bear with me if you would.

00:47:32.330 --> 00:47:39.000
We come to pace of change, and how we move
from current allocations to future allocations,

00:47:39.000 --> 00:47:44.940
nearing as closely and as rapidly as possible
the target allocations revealed by the things

00:47:44.940 --> 00:47:46.630
we have just been talking about.

00:47:46.630 --> 00:47:52.000
Let me remind you of some of our particular
policies, which are frankly taken forward

00:47:52.000 --> 00:47:58.450
from last year and given additional power
through the five-year allocations that we

00:47:58.450 --> 00:47:59.890
are about to discuss.

00:47:59.890 --> 00:48:05.290
Firstly, we do want to bring all CCGs to target
funding over time, and specifically, we have

00:48:05.290 --> 00:48:10.220
a commitment which we have made to ourselves
and to the POC, I would say, to bring those

00:48:10.220 --> 00:48:19.510
who are more than 5% under target rapidly
– i.e., next year – to being within 5%

00:48:19.510 --> 00:48:20.850
of the target.

00:48:20.850 --> 00:48:21.990
That is a commitment we made last year.

00:48:21.990 --> 00:48:26.130
I am pleased to say that if you accept either
of the variants of the proposals that we have

00:48:26.130 --> 00:48:30.670
in this section, that will be delivered; which
is a great relief to Simon and me, because

00:48:30.670 --> 00:48:33.420
otherwise we would have to go to the POC and
explain why we were not doing it, but it is

00:48:33.420 --> 00:48:35.390
an important thing for us to do.

00:48:35.390 --> 00:48:39.770
Equally, we have set out on a journey of bringing
primary care into alignment with target formulae,

00:48:39.770 --> 00:48:43.600
and we are continuing that journey in a way
I will describe in a minute.

00:48:43.600 --> 00:48:47.360
In doing all of that, though, it comes back
to my ‘and/ands’ earlier on; we need to

00:48:47.360 --> 00:48:52.340
make sure that we are mindful of value for
money and ability to adapt at pace.

00:48:52.340 --> 00:48:56.350
In constructing the rules of engagement around
pace of change, we have been very mindful

00:48:56.350 --> 00:49:02.350
last year and this year of: those who get
additional growth, how much can they meaningfully

00:49:02.350 --> 00:49:05.950
invest and achieve value for money on?

00:49:05.950 --> 00:49:10.180
Equally importantly, those who have lower
increases because they are overfunded, how

00:49:10.180 --> 00:49:18.430
quickly can they adapt their spending patterns
to come in line with the future allocations?

00:49:18.430 --> 00:49:25.530
That gives us, I think, a variety of considerations
as we go through the next phase.

00:49:25.530 --> 00:49:28.500
The additional complexity this time – so
up to now, we have always been talking about

00:49:28.500 --> 00:49:32.650
individual allocations in how we do pace of
change – the additional thing is that we

00:49:32.650 --> 00:49:37.810
have to achieve the other ‘and/and’, which
is to get the equity right: the equity of

00:49:37.810 --> 00:49:43.380
allocations at total place, which is where
all of the things for a population come together,

00:49:43.380 --> 00:49:46.700
at the same time as keeping the job for individual
commissioning streams doable.

00:49:46.700 --> 00:49:52.270
You cannot have a CCG that cannot do its job
because its total place allocation is brilliant

00:49:52.270 --> 00:49:56.410
but it is all in the wrong part of it, so
we have had to manage both levels of that

00:49:56.410 --> 00:49:59.080
in the proposals that we have here.

00:49:59.080 --> 00:50:03.360
There are four ways set out in the paper that
we could think of doing that.

00:50:03.360 --> 00:50:06.580
The first is to look at each individual commissioning
stream individually and continue to do what

00:50:06.580 --> 00:50:07.580
we have always done.

00:50:07.580 --> 00:50:11.980
I guess I have not presented that as a very
credible alternative, and it is not, so it

00:50:11.980 --> 00:50:13.690
is there for completeness.

00:50:13.690 --> 00:50:17.110
The second thing we could do is just to ignore
the individual streams and go to the aggregate

00:50:17.110 --> 00:50:20.250
level, get all those allocations right and
then move forward.

00:50:20.250 --> 00:50:22.790
That does not really work either, whether
or not you include specialised commissioning.

00:50:22.790 --> 00:50:27.840
Frankly, I have tried to dress it up as four
options, but there is only one that fulfils

00:50:27.840 --> 00:50:33.840
the criterion that I set, if you managed to
notice that one, which is the hybrid option,

00:50:33.840 --> 00:50:40.420
which seeks to both get the total place allocation
to where we need to get it, but sets some

00:50:40.420 --> 00:50:44.730
rules, if you like, for the individual components,
which ensures that CCGs will still be able

00:50:44.730 --> 00:50:49.050
to commission sensibly with the allocations
they have for their part of the total place,

00:50:49.050 --> 00:50:51.690
and similarly for primary care and specialised.

00:50:51.690 --> 00:50:53.510
So I commend that to you.

00:50:53.510 --> 00:50:59.210
Paragraph 49, the reasons for including specialised
are rehearsed, because it is our conviction

00:50:59.210 --> 00:51:03.990
that it is important we move forward with
the specialised allocations as part of the

00:51:03.990 --> 00:51:06.310
place-based set of changes.

00:51:06.310 --> 00:51:10.970
The major reason, and if necessary the only
reason, is it is simply inequitable to give

00:51:10.970 --> 00:51:17.850
people CCG allocations which take no account
of whether they underuse, overuse or whatever,

00:51:17.850 --> 00:51:19.270
specialised services.

00:51:19.270 --> 00:51:22.240
Which is effectively what we have been doing
today; we have been blind to that, partly

00:51:22.240 --> 00:51:24.190
because we have not had the data to do it
with.

00:51:24.190 --> 00:51:28.420
It is inequitable to do that, so therefore
the total place including specialised starts

00:51:28.420 --> 00:51:34.270
to bring in the holistic view of where the
total spend across a population is taking

00:51:34.270 --> 00:51:39.780
place, and enables us to take that into account
in setting the future allocations.

00:51:39.780 --> 00:51:44.070
The second thing is, it will support the co-commissioning
that Noel quite helpfully brought out in the

00:51:44.070 --> 00:51:46.870
earlier discussion, if we bring all of that
together.

00:51:46.870 --> 00:51:51.510
The third thing to say is that it will give
us the transparency that we need.

00:51:51.510 --> 00:51:52.620
As I say, we have been a bit blind to that.

00:51:52.620 --> 00:51:57.660
The fourth thing is, this is a moveable feast
in the sense that we are refining continuously

00:51:57.660 --> 00:51:59.230
what specialised commissioning is.

00:51:59.230 --> 00:52:04.200
We are moving forward with trying to get greater
consistency across the country, for example,

00:52:04.200 --> 00:52:09.120
with the rules by which we identify what is
a specialised activity and what is not, and

00:52:09.120 --> 00:52:14.810
over time there is a likelihood that we will
change the balance in small or bigger ways

00:52:14.810 --> 00:52:17.600
between specialised in a careful and planned
way.

00:52:17.600 --> 00:52:21.480
However, if we do not have all of the allocations
coming together in the way that the pressure

00:52:21.480 --> 00:52:25.550
is set in the system, each of those changes
will be a struggle because the money will

00:52:25.550 --> 00:52:28.410
be in the wrong place, and we will have to
revisit our allocations.

00:52:28.410 --> 00:52:32.810
So I commend this to you as a futureproofing
of our strategy, for allocations as well as

00:52:32.810 --> 00:52:36.550
something which is a fundamental equity issue.

00:52:36.550 --> 00:52:37.620
There are some downsides.

00:52:37.620 --> 00:52:42.960
As I have mentioned, the formula is quite
a complex one, and it does not yet have ACRA

00:52:42.960 --> 00:52:44.250
approval.

00:52:44.250 --> 00:52:46.130
That brings with it a degree of risk.

00:52:46.130 --> 00:52:49.680
In the paper, we talk about the fact that
the partial service coverage is simply the

00:52:49.680 --> 00:52:53.760
50% for which we do not have a proper formula,
and for which we have therefore done without

00:52:53.760 --> 00:52:56.780
a needs-based formula, just on actuals.

00:52:56.780 --> 00:53:00.600
My argument to you would be that that actually
dampens the risk rather than increasing the

00:53:00.600 --> 00:53:03.790
risk, because there is part of it – and
actually, the more volatile part of it – which

00:53:03.790 --> 00:53:09.100
we are not bringing a pace of change element
to at this stage until we have a robust way

00:53:09.100 --> 00:53:10.720
to do that.

00:53:10.720 --> 00:53:14.330
I make the argument there that we should proceed
including specialised.

00:53:14.330 --> 00:53:19.110
We are quite happy obviously to discuss that
when we get to the end of this.

00:53:19.110 --> 00:53:22.660
There are then three steps to holiness in
this particular one.

00:53:22.660 --> 00:53:28.590
The first one is to apply those basic rules
to each of the commissioning streams to ensure

00:53:28.590 --> 00:53:35.770
that the basic requirements to run a CCG allocation,
a primary medical care allocation and a specialised

00:53:35.770 --> 00:53:37.960
allocation are in place.

00:53:37.960 --> 00:53:42.960
That does not consume all of the growth that
we have for each of these commissioning streams,

00:53:42.960 --> 00:53:48.180
and therefore, we take the growth capacity
that we have after that step and model it

00:53:48.180 --> 00:53:54.330
at the total place stage to get a total pace
of change introduced into the system.

00:53:54.330 --> 00:54:00.420
Having got a total pace of change sorted out,
we then have a third step, which is to pass

00:54:00.420 --> 00:54:06.000
all of that money back down into the three
constituent allocations: the CCG allocation,

00:54:06.000 --> 00:54:08.710
the primary medical care and the specialised
care.

00:54:08.710 --> 00:54:13.750
If that sounds complex, it is a little complex,
but it is the best way to reconcile those

00:54:13.750 --> 00:54:18.041
two priorities of having total place-based
equity and allocations we can work with at

00:54:18.041 --> 00:54:21.050
the individual levels.

00:54:21.050 --> 00:54:24.520
All of the rules that we have applied in the
first of those stages, and indeed the second

00:54:24.520 --> 00:54:28.340
of those stages, derive directly from the
things we discussed last year.

00:54:28.340 --> 00:54:33.940
A priority to get people no more than 5% under
target is enacted as a basic rule in each

00:54:33.940 --> 00:54:35.190
of those streams.

00:54:35.190 --> 00:54:41.170
Secondly, we have minimum per capita growth,
which ensures in particular that those who

00:54:41.170 --> 00:54:48.160
are growing fast, which was one of our concerns
last year, are getting a growing allocation

00:54:48.160 --> 00:54:49.620
as this goes through.

00:54:49.620 --> 00:54:54.210
Thirdly, that every CCG gets a minimum level
of cash growth, in other words the absolute

00:54:54.210 --> 00:54:58.810
allocation that they get, which to some extent
gives protection for slow-growing CCGs and

00:54:58.810 --> 00:55:00.820
gives them a chance to adapt over time.

00:55:00.820 --> 00:55:05.950
To a more important extent in this particular
context, we have ensured that CCGs in particular,

00:55:05.950 --> 00:55:09.650
and primary care actually, have enough money
to deal with the pensions pressures that I

00:55:09.650 --> 00:55:15.960
talked about in year one, in 2016/2017, irrespective
of all the other pace of change that we do.

00:55:15.960 --> 00:55:20.680
Then when we put all of the seven-day service
money out in the allocations, which, in this

00:55:20.680 --> 00:55:25.579
model is in 2020/2021 – we will see whether
it comes out earlier than that, but by 2020/2021

00:55:25.579 --> 00:55:28.580
– clearly it will be important that every
CCG has the wherewithal to pay for seven-day

00:55:28.580 --> 00:55:31.140
services, so we have added that in.

00:55:31.140 --> 00:55:33.640
That is what that little second step does.

00:55:33.640 --> 00:55:37.760
The last one is the one where we have made
a bit of a change in the proposals to you.

00:55:37.760 --> 00:55:42.310
Last year, if you remember, we capped the
growth for those who are more than 5% over

00:55:42.310 --> 00:55:48.180
target, those who are receiving each year
significantly more than the target says that

00:55:48.180 --> 00:55:52.990
the needs of their population, both met and
unmet, should demand.

00:55:52.990 --> 00:55:58.580
We capped that at effectively real terms growth
last year, no more than real-terms growth,

00:55:58.580 --> 00:56:01.740
even if they had growing populations.

00:56:01.740 --> 00:56:05.390
That is not getting us traction, frankly,
in bringing their over-targetedness – if

00:56:05.390 --> 00:56:08.040
there is such a word – down to sensible
levels.

00:56:08.040 --> 00:56:12.890
So in the proposals we have here, at least
in option one, we are going to – particularly

00:56:12.890 --> 00:56:19.140
for those who are over 10% over target – bring
that down to effectively flat cash, plus those

00:56:19.140 --> 00:56:23.700
specific pressures that I talked about, in
order to accelerate the pace in a reasonable

00:56:23.700 --> 00:56:28.310
way, but nonetheless accelerate the pace of
those organisations; some of which are 20%,

00:56:28.310 --> 00:56:34.540
30% over target, and will never, frankly,
get back to any alignment with their needs-based

00:56:34.540 --> 00:56:40.520
formula unless we accelerate that in the way
that I have described in the paper.

00:56:40.520 --> 00:56:41.590
Those are the rules for the CCGs.

00:56:41.590 --> 00:56:46.561
There are similar rules for the primary care
medical allocations; given that they are as

00:56:46.561 --> 00:56:50.140
similar as they are, I will not repeat all
I have just said in relation to that.

00:56:50.140 --> 00:56:54.820
In specialised, we have in this first instance
no particular rules other than that everyone

00:56:54.820 --> 00:56:59.070
gets the same per capita growth, because given
the volatility of specialised, we think that

00:56:59.070 --> 00:57:00.660
is the best way to handle it.

00:57:00.660 --> 00:57:04.780
I will come back to its impact in the total
in a minute.

00:57:04.780 --> 00:57:08.960
That is the first stage; that gets you to
basic allocations for CCGs, primary medical

00:57:08.960 --> 00:57:10.660
and specialised.

00:57:10.660 --> 00:57:14.340
We then turn to second of those and apply
a similar set of rules to the total capacity

00:57:14.340 --> 00:57:22.400
we have at place-based level, total of those
three, in order to discharge the total capacity

00:57:22.400 --> 00:57:23.930
we have for growth funding.

00:57:23.930 --> 00:57:30.170
That will channel it to those who, on a total
place basis, are most underfunded, irrespective

00:57:30.170 --> 00:57:33.310
of whether they are underfunded in the individual
components or whether they have mixes.

00:57:33.310 --> 00:57:39.610
We have every possible mix across the country
of underfunded CCGs, under and overfunded

00:57:39.610 --> 00:57:45.130
GP services, and a similar dispersion of specialised
commissioning.

00:57:45.130 --> 00:57:49.540
The end result here is the most equitable
way to make sure that, in total place-based

00:57:49.540 --> 00:57:54.280
terms, the people who are most underfunded
get the biggest growth in that particular

00:57:54.280 --> 00:57:55.280
dimension.

00:57:55.280 --> 00:57:57.030
That is the second part of it.

00:57:57.030 --> 00:58:01.770
Then we have rules set out in paragraph 53
as to how that goes back to the individual

00:58:01.770 --> 00:58:02.770
component allocations.

00:58:02.770 --> 00:58:06.470
I will let you read those for yourself; it
effectually tries to get the money to the

00:58:06.470 --> 00:58:11.640
part of the allocation which is most underfunded,
which I think you would find a logical way

00:58:11.640 --> 00:58:15.140
to try and give the capacity that that results
in.

00:58:15.140 --> 00:58:20.280
We will spend a lot of time communicating,
I can assure you, with CCGs and with our own

00:58:20.280 --> 00:58:23.970
teams about exactly how all of that works
and why it makes sense in the end, because

00:58:23.970 --> 00:58:28.920
it is easier to do with a set of worked examples
than it is in a set of paragraphs in a Board

00:58:28.920 --> 00:58:29.920
paper.

00:58:29.920 --> 00:58:35.860
Rest assured, the methodology works and will
be communicated in that way.

00:58:35.860 --> 00:58:39.060
We are offering you two alternatives really,
one of which is what I have just described,

00:58:39.060 --> 00:58:40.300
which is option one.

00:58:40.300 --> 00:58:46.860
There is a second option, which is to be less
redistributive at the overfunded end, and

00:58:46.860 --> 00:58:51.190
to revert effectively back to what we did
last year, which is to continue to protect

00:58:51.190 --> 00:58:55.890
in real terms the allocations to those who
are significantly overfunded.

00:58:55.890 --> 00:59:00.980
The consequence you can see on the following
page, which I will not take you through every

00:59:00.980 --> 00:59:05.440
number on, but just to give you a sense of
the differential performance.

00:59:05.440 --> 00:59:10.270
This shows at the top the DFT, the distance
from target distribution.

00:59:10.270 --> 00:59:15.910
The simplest way I can explain it is that,
as of 2016/2017, if you see the green blocks

00:59:15.910 --> 00:59:21.590
on the chart, that says that there is no CCG
and no total place in the country which is

00:59:21.590 --> 00:59:24.930
more than 5% under target in the proposals.

00:59:24.930 --> 00:59:30.710
That is true of either of the proposals, both
the more accelerated and the less accelerated.

00:59:30.710 --> 00:59:34.230
You can see we get to the point, a rather
exciting point, in 2020/2021, if that is not

00:59:34.230 --> 00:59:41.190
too far to look ahead, where on a total place
basis we do not even have any CCGs that are

00:59:41.190 --> 00:59:47.050
more than 2.5% under target, which I think
is a worthy next step to take once we have

00:59:47.050 --> 00:59:50.870
conquered the Everest of getting to within
5%.

00:59:50.870 --> 00:59:54.780
You can see that there is significant convergence
in the top section.

00:59:54.780 --> 00:59:59.300
I would draw to your attention, for example,
in 2018/2019, which is the end of the firm

00:59:59.300 --> 01:00:06.200
allocations we are doing, 164 in the middle,
you can see CCGs are within 2.5% either side

01:00:06.200 --> 01:00:07.200
of allocation.

01:00:07.200 --> 01:00:12.840
That is the best part of 80% of CCGs in the
central zone around the target allocations;

01:00:12.840 --> 01:00:15.930
and as you can see, that extends as we go
forward.

01:00:15.930 --> 01:00:18.880
So it does what it is intended to do.

01:00:18.880 --> 01:00:25.070
You can see in the middle block the range
of growth that is applied to various CCGs.

01:00:25.070 --> 01:00:31.100
In 2016/2017, it is noticeable that a number
of CCGs will get significant amounts of growth.

01:00:31.100 --> 01:00:35.680
They are a relatively small number of CCGs
that get the sort of growth that are in the

01:00:35.680 --> 01:00:41.330
maximum; lest CCG finance directors now be
running their hands together and looking forward

01:00:41.330 --> 01:00:46.890
to receiving a cheque for 10.79% growth, that
is one CCG which is massively under target

01:00:46.890 --> 01:00:49.090
which would get that.

01:00:49.090 --> 01:00:50.210
That is the span of it.

01:00:50.210 --> 01:00:53.720
You can see the mean, median and the minima,
if that sort of thing interests you.

01:00:53.720 --> 01:00:58.170
There is more capacity in 2016/2017 to do
things than there is in the middle years.

01:00:58.170 --> 01:01:04.060
In 2020/2021, again, we have a slightly bigger
dispersion of the numbers.

01:01:04.060 --> 01:01:07.650
If I contrast that with option two, which
you are invited to look at, you end up with

01:01:07.650 --> 01:01:10.980
more people, as you would expect, who are
significantly overfunded at the end of the

01:01:10.980 --> 01:01:11.980
period, inevitably.

01:01:11.980 --> 01:01:17.280
In fact, we do not really make much inroad
in that at all because it is simply such a

01:01:17.280 --> 01:01:19.660
big degree of difference that many of these
CCGs have.

01:01:19.660 --> 01:01:23.220
We just need to start to deal with that.

01:01:23.220 --> 01:01:29.550
You can see also that my wonderful goal of
being within 2.5% or no more than 2.5% under

01:01:29.550 --> 01:01:31.350
target does not get achieved in the final
year.

01:01:31.350 --> 01:01:34.930
I would say that is by a small difference,
actually, so I would not overstate that.

01:01:34.930 --> 01:01:39.390
It is also worth saying the financial differences
between the top and the bottom table are not

01:01:39.390 --> 01:01:40.390
enormous.

01:01:40.390 --> 01:01:44.900
We are talking about less than £100 million
that moves, as it were, between those two,

01:01:44.900 --> 01:01:48.720
because there is a relatively small number
of CCGs that are more than 5%, as you can

01:01:48.720 --> 01:01:49.940
see, over target.

01:01:49.940 --> 01:01:55.670
So I would not want to overstate the differences
between options one and two.

01:01:55.670 --> 01:01:59.140
It is my judgement – which is why we are
recommending option one – that if we want

01:01:59.140 --> 01:02:02.910
to deal with an equitable settlement, we need
to get the money as quickly as possible to

01:02:02.910 --> 01:02:07.540
those who are underfunded, and we cannot leave
a situation where we have some CCGs who are

01:02:07.540 --> 01:02:13.070
20% or 30% overfunded and there is no inroad
being made into that.

01:02:13.070 --> 01:02:17.400
I would say that most of the ones that are
significantly overfunded do not appear very

01:02:17.400 --> 01:02:22.350
often on my list of CCGs that I need to refer
to in the deficit part of the table.

01:02:22.350 --> 01:02:27.460
It is good not to be in deficit, but if you
are not in deficit because of that level of

01:02:27.460 --> 01:02:31.580
overfunding, that is perhaps not something
that we can leave intact for very much longer,

01:02:31.580 --> 01:02:35.690
so that is the recommendation I would make
to you.

01:02:35.690 --> 01:02:39.390
I think that is probably all I want to say.

01:02:39.390 --> 01:02:43.400
There are some comments in there about primary
care; effectively things that we can do to

01:02:43.400 --> 01:02:48.380
add to the transparency of what we have done,
to show it in the context of the other bits

01:02:48.380 --> 01:02:52.790
of primary care which are not part of what
I have just described, how we can look at

01:02:52.790 --> 01:02:55.240
social care and public health on top of that.

01:02:55.240 --> 01:03:00.590
There is a long consideration of whether we
should be factoring the interesting fate of

01:03:00.590 --> 01:03:01.920
social care into all of this.

01:03:01.920 --> 01:03:05.220
Even if we wanted to, we could not, because
we do not have the data to do that; and indeed,

01:03:05.220 --> 01:03:08.660
the data does not yet exist about the impact
of social care around the place.

01:03:08.660 --> 01:03:12.630
However, we do not believe it to be appropriate
in any case to start changing allocations

01:03:12.630 --> 01:03:18.820
for the health system in response to potentially
very uneven sets of social care challenges

01:03:18.820 --> 01:03:20.380
around the country.

01:03:20.380 --> 01:03:24.120
My recommendation is that we build as much
transparency around the place-based allocations

01:03:24.120 --> 01:03:30.500
as we can, but we stick to the already complex
enough set of things that we have just talked

01:03:30.500 --> 01:03:31.500
about.

01:03:31.500 --> 01:03:34.720
May I just pause for breath and inspiration
at that point?

01:03:34.720 --> 01:03:40.150
If there are, again, any questions or observations
about pace of change, the choice between those

01:03:40.150 --> 01:03:44.260
two options, now would be a good time to raise
it.

01:03:44.260 --> 01:03:51.860
Right, let me invite any questions from colleagues
on the Board.

01:03:51.860 --> 01:03:54.390
It is an opinion really, rather than a question.

01:03:54.390 --> 01:04:06.300
I think if we want to end up with no CCG more
than 2.5% underfunded or 66 more than 2.5%,

01:04:06.300 --> 01:04:11.830
and it is a relatively small balance, why
on earth would we not want to do that?

01:04:11.830 --> 01:04:15.060
For me, it seems obvious; and by the way,
we have five years to do it.

01:04:15.060 --> 01:04:20.160
It does not feel a reckless pace to me; it
just feels slightly more effort.

01:04:20.160 --> 01:04:28.830
Can I just add one question on that, which
is: to what extent, when we are capping the

01:04:28.830 --> 01:04:38.960
growth in those who have more than 5% overfunded,
are there areas of real deprivation or areas

01:04:38.960 --> 01:04:44.280
where you mention that they do not feature
on your degree of deficit?

01:04:44.280 --> 01:04:46.800
It is only four or so.

01:04:46.800 --> 01:04:51.820
From 20, we are talking about eight over the
three-year time horizon, so it is not a huge

01:04:51.820 --> 01:04:56.110
number of CCGs we are talking about here,
I think.

01:04:56.110 --> 01:05:02.470
You can assure us that there is nothing very
difficult going on there in terms of areas

01:05:02.470 --> 01:05:03.880
of high deprivation?

01:05:03.880 --> 01:05:08.720
I can give you an assurance, I think, that
finance is not a reason why those areas are

01:05:08.720 --> 01:05:12.610
struggling, and that what we are changing
here would not pose an unmanageable burden

01:05:12.610 --> 01:05:13.610
on them.

01:05:13.610 --> 01:05:24.250
First of all, I would like to say that is
formidable: not only the amount of work, but

01:05:24.250 --> 01:05:27.900
also your grasp of the detail is truly impressive.

01:05:27.900 --> 01:05:34.120
The issue of linking specialised services,
which we have a special interest in as Board

01:05:34.120 --> 01:05:39.830
members, to CCGs is obviously a complex one,
and you have used the word ‘judgement’

01:05:39.830 --> 01:05:44.330
once or twice, which implies that there will
be others who will disagree with some of these

01:05:44.330 --> 01:05:45.330
judgements.

01:05:45.330 --> 01:05:49.250
One of the thoughts that occurred to me as
you were speaking was that some of the specialised

01:05:49.250 --> 01:05:50.970
services are patchy.

01:05:50.970 --> 01:05:56.690
One could imagine scenarios, for example,
hepatitis C, being more concentrated in some

01:05:56.690 --> 01:06:01.830
CCGs than others, or certain rare diseases
which are phenomenally expensive clustering

01:06:01.830 --> 01:06:04.960
around families in one very localised area.

01:06:04.960 --> 01:06:13.200
To what extent will we have the flexibility
to address that sort of lack of averaging,

01:06:13.200 --> 01:06:15.340
which you have had to use in your original
calculation?

01:06:15.340 --> 01:06:16.340
Very good question.

01:06:16.340 --> 01:06:21.050
I did not draw out that particular detail
in what I said.

01:06:21.050 --> 01:06:22.880
Two levels of answer to that.

01:06:22.880 --> 01:06:28.140
Firstly, there is a variety of services which
is so specialised and uneven in the way you

01:06:28.140 --> 01:06:33.010
describe it, that we manage them nationally
and, as far as I can conceive, will continue

01:06:33.010 --> 01:06:36.840
to manage them nationally, so they do not
really come into this set of equations.

01:06:36.840 --> 01:06:40.540
We have excluded those, effectively, from
the place-based allocations.

01:06:40.540 --> 01:06:42.990
This is quite an important detail which I
did not mention.

01:06:42.990 --> 01:06:47.060
We are only in the allocations looking at
the bits that we would not manage nationally.

01:06:47.060 --> 01:06:50.580
There are in varying degrees then, within
the portfolio – that does come into question

01:06:50.580 --> 01:06:54.770
for, if you like, total place perspective
– things that you would almost certainly

01:06:54.770 --> 01:07:00.710
choose then to aggregate as a set of commissioners
getting involved in co commissioning.

01:07:00.710 --> 01:07:05.080
Clearly, at the moment, we do it all in reasonably
aggregated hubs, and my assumption would be

01:07:05.080 --> 01:07:09.470
that, in future construction, some services
would be best at least done on that basis;

01:07:09.470 --> 01:07:13.180
and as we have already reflected, we do not
have concrete plans to change the basis of

01:07:13.180 --> 01:07:14.480
that anyway.

01:07:14.480 --> 01:07:17.870
The sorts of examples that you have talked
about – hep C, for example, is one that

01:07:17.870 --> 01:07:23.400
in all foreseeable circumstances, at the moment
at least, we would retain in the central clump,

01:07:23.400 --> 01:07:26.710
if that is not a technical term too many.

01:07:26.710 --> 01:07:31.850
First of all, could I echo John’s praise?

01:07:31.850 --> 01:07:38.850
I thought in spite of the complexity of what
you were trying to explain, it came over really

01:07:38.850 --> 01:07:45.540
very, very clearly for us as members of the
Board, and I am hoping that it came over similarly

01:07:45.540 --> 01:07:48.340
clearly for members of the public.

01:07:48.340 --> 01:07:53.720
I would anticipate in due course that there
will be a sort of simplified version put up

01:07:53.720 --> 01:07:56.990
onto our information channels.

01:07:56.990 --> 01:08:05.270
What I was going to ask is really in connection
with the timetable, the CCGs consultation

01:08:05.270 --> 01:08:08.600
coming back in January, and I am actually
very pleased that you are actually moving

01:08:08.600 --> 01:08:14.800
that timeline forward to allow them to digest
their Christmas pudding and come back later.

01:08:14.800 --> 01:08:22.859
With ACRA having to look at the formula for
specialised commissioning and the risk that

01:08:22.859 --> 01:08:29.980
is baked into that; given the importance,
as Noel has pointed out, of the specialised

01:08:29.980 --> 01:08:42.060
commissioning elements and the co-commissioning
push, encouragement, what might the ACRA response

01:08:42.060 --> 01:08:49.540
do to the timetable if ACRA comes back pointing
out distortions or anything that we need to

01:08:49.540 --> 01:08:50.540
deal with?

01:08:50.540 --> 01:08:54.170
They will not affect the timetable because
we are not going to wait for ACRA to do its

01:08:54.170 --> 01:08:56.630
work before we assure the numbers, so they
are not going to do this before the first

01:08:56.630 --> 01:08:58.530
week of January, to be clear.

01:08:58.530 --> 01:09:03.920
We will issue the allocations, subject to
any changes that you might wish to make, on

01:09:03.920 --> 01:09:09.510
the basis that we have set out here, and on
the assumption that ACRA will find what we

01:09:09.510 --> 01:09:14.660
have done to be accurate and appropriate.

01:09:14.660 --> 01:09:19.790
I think there are two possible negative scenarios;
not ones that I anticipate, but ones that

01:09:19.790 --> 01:09:21.670
we have to be prepared for.

01:09:21.670 --> 01:09:25.290
One is that there is a suggestion from ACRA
that there is a particular aspect of it that

01:09:25.290 --> 01:09:29.820
could have been done differently – better,
a refinement to what we have done that would

01:09:29.820 --> 01:09:33.799
be appropriate – and I think in those circumstances
we would need to look very carefully at the

01:09:33.799 --> 01:09:38.000
magnitude of that, and whether it really required
a change before the next time we would do

01:09:38.000 --> 01:09:41.490
binding allocations, which would be three
years off.

01:09:41.490 --> 01:09:44.620
I think if ACRA came back and said that the
whole thing is misguided, a) I would be a

01:09:44.620 --> 01:09:47.040
bit disappointed, because we have had quite
a lot of discussion with them at this stage,

01:09:47.040 --> 01:09:51.610
but b) if there is a major thing, we will
have to rethink.

01:09:51.610 --> 01:09:57.200
My expectation though would be, if there is
such a thing, we could manage that by some

01:09:57.200 --> 01:10:01.870
kind of non-recurrent adjustment in 2016/2017
if needed, that could adjust beyond that.

01:10:01.870 --> 01:10:06.130
As I have already mentioned, there will be
other things that we will do as we go into

01:10:06.130 --> 01:10:11.800
2017/2018 and onwards which will potentially
put additional money into the local system;

01:10:11.800 --> 01:10:15.780
for example, if we can find ways in which
some of the elements of transformation can

01:10:15.780 --> 01:10:20.640
be decentralised, if I can put it that way,
into allocations, that would offer an opportunity

01:10:20.640 --> 01:10:25.610
if there were also some adjustments that were
required from the ACRA review to kind of wheel

01:10:25.610 --> 01:10:26.610
them into that.

01:10:26.610 --> 01:10:31.180
As I say, my expectation – and it is nearly
Christmas, so I can wish for it as well, I

01:10:31.180 --> 01:10:36.990
suppose – is that we will have a smooth
ride with this and it will go through, but

01:10:36.990 --> 01:10:48.820
I would not want in any way to prejudge what
ACRA forms as its view in due course.

01:10:48.820 --> 01:10:55.210
Paul, to contextualise the choice between
the options in terms of what this means for

01:10:55.210 --> 01:11:02.030
patients, in terms of access to GP services,
access to emergency and acute, access to mental

01:11:02.030 --> 01:11:10.290
health, quality of outcomes; at first sight,
the U-shaped profile of the per capita means

01:11:10.290 --> 01:11:15.890
are less than two decimal points different
between the options.

01:11:15.890 --> 01:11:21.330
How do you characterise the real difference
in what patients would see between our choice

01:11:21.330 --> 01:11:26.300
between pace of change option one and pace
of change option two?

01:11:26.300 --> 01:11:32.490
I think I have to base it on the fact that
the best way to get value in the health system

01:11:32.490 --> 01:11:37.190
is to match money to where the need is, because
that will get the money working hardest for

01:11:37.190 --> 01:11:40.580
patients who are generating that need.

01:11:40.580 --> 01:11:45.660
There are in underfunded CCGs at the moment
clearly needs which cannot be being fulfilled,

01:11:45.660 --> 01:11:50.281
because they do not have the money to do it,
and either they are showing a deficit – which

01:11:50.281 --> 01:11:54.190
frankly just takes the money back from other
parts of the health system – or there are

01:11:54.190 --> 01:11:59.230
services which are not being provided at the
cost, and therefore the delivery, which one

01:11:59.230 --> 01:12:00.230
would expect.

01:12:00.230 --> 01:12:06.650
So I think dealing with the underfundedness
of CCGs enables them to make the money work

01:12:06.650 --> 01:12:08.200
in fulfilling the needs of patients.

01:12:08.200 --> 01:12:12.850
As I have already commented, I think whilst
there is doubtless a mixed picture amongst

01:12:12.850 --> 01:12:17.320
those at the other end of the spectrum, there
are a number where the money is simply not

01:12:17.320 --> 01:12:21.670
working hard enough, because it has not needed
to, to be frank about it, and I think we need

01:12:21.670 --> 01:12:26.260
to get the alignment so that every CCG has
the same challenge of using the right amount

01:12:26.260 --> 01:12:30.920
of money for its group of patients to deliver
the maximum possible benefit.

01:12:30.920 --> 01:12:37.770
Paul, may I just ask: there are a couple of
paragraphs here where you are not asking us

01:12:37.770 --> 01:12:43.650
for a decision, paragraphs 61 and 62, which
are about the combined financial control total

01:12:43.650 --> 01:12:47.820
for the local health economy, and likewise
for CCGs working together.

01:12:47.820 --> 01:12:53.100
It would be quite interesting just to have
from you a rather more developed understanding

01:12:53.100 --> 01:12:59.920
of how this in turn would advance the principles
that lie behind the allocations you are inviting

01:12:59.920 --> 01:13:01.720
us to make.

01:13:01.720 --> 01:13:07.710
There are really three aspects of the three
paragraphs, imaginatively enough, one of which

01:13:07.710 --> 01:13:11.980
is a fairly simple thing, which is: we are
giving quite a bit of additional money to

01:13:11.980 --> 01:13:15.630
some CCGs, indeed a number of CCGs.

01:13:15.630 --> 01:13:18.790
We need to be absolutely certain that that
money is going to be spent on plans which

01:13:18.790 --> 01:13:23.870
have the robustness and all the benefits which
Noel was trying to get at, I think, and not

01:13:23.870 --> 01:13:27.600
just more money which is then being badly
directed.

01:13:27.600 --> 01:13:32.240
So the linkage we have made there is that
the quality of the integrated plans being

01:13:32.240 --> 01:13:37.790
done in the summer in those CCGs and indeed
in all CCGs, will be linked to the degree

01:13:37.790 --> 01:13:43.440
to which they earn the allocations theoretically
allocated in this particular paper.

01:13:43.440 --> 01:13:47.250
I think it is really important from a value-for-money
perspective, it is really important also as

01:13:47.250 --> 01:13:53.610
a concentrating feature, to ensure that the
right incentives are in place for CCGs to

01:13:53.610 --> 01:13:58.050
engage with that process and be successful
in it, with all the support that we are going

01:13:58.050 --> 01:14:00.430
to be giving them to do that.

01:14:00.430 --> 01:14:04.790
As I said earlier, it mirrors the incentives
for providers to engage with that through

01:14:04.790 --> 01:14:06.770
the Sustainability Funds.

01:14:06.770 --> 01:14:12.290
The second proposal, which, I have to say,
is a reasonably recent piece of thought development

01:14:12.290 --> 01:14:16.770
which we will need to work on more, is the
shared financial control total, but it comes

01:14:16.770 --> 01:14:21.310
from a philosophy at least that what we are
trying to achieve here is health economies

01:14:21.310 --> 01:14:25.710
which are as robust and vibrant as possible,
as opposed to individual organisations in

01:14:25.710 --> 01:14:32.730
health economies that, certainly in the worst
cases, compete for the financial space and

01:14:32.730 --> 01:14:34.670
the life of the health economy.

01:14:34.670 --> 01:14:38.460
This would encourage those who are going into
rather more collaborative modes of working

01:14:38.460 --> 01:14:43.940
together for finance not to be a barrier to
that by them being held to individual control

01:14:43.940 --> 01:14:44.940
totals.

01:14:44.940 --> 01:14:48.770
I have to say, there is a lot more thought
that needs to go into that; because, for example,

01:14:48.770 --> 01:14:54.310
we have accountability for the entire financial
performance of the commissioning system, and

01:14:54.310 --> 01:14:55.710
that is something we need to deliver.

01:14:55.710 --> 01:14:59.580
So these need to be first thought through,
and second, piloted, and then we will need

01:14:59.580 --> 01:15:04.270
to work out, if it becomes anything more than
a set of selective pilots, how we adapt the

01:15:04.270 --> 01:15:06.020
overall financial regime.

01:15:06.020 --> 01:15:09.760
As I have said in the paper, that would require
further discussion; not just here, but with

01:15:09.760 --> 01:15:14.421
partners in NHS Improvement and in central
government, who clearly have a responsibility

01:15:14.421 --> 01:15:15.900
for the financial regime.

01:15:15.900 --> 01:15:21.810
I think that could be a real liberating force
if we can make that work, to getting the collaboration

01:15:21.810 --> 01:15:26.620
between providers and commissioners to the
peak that we need it to be if we are going

01:15:26.620 --> 01:15:28.270
to deliver on the difficult challenges.

01:15:28.270 --> 01:15:32.400
Then the third one is quite an exciting one
which I had contemplated in my London days

01:15:32.400 --> 01:15:36.670
and never quite got round to doing, and which
I know London has been doing quite a bit of

01:15:36.670 --> 01:15:40.330
thinking about this – is not uniquely a
London problem, but it is perhaps best exemplified

01:15:40.330 --> 01:15:45.941
by London – which is, if there are parts
of the country which, by mutual consent, want

01:15:45.941 --> 01:15:51.320
to make sure that they get more quickly to
money lined up to where it belongs, we would

01:15:51.320 --> 01:15:54.640
want to be facilitating of that rather than
people stopping that happening and saying,

01:15:54.640 --> 01:15:58.520
‘Sorry, we have made up the allocations;
you have to stick precisely to it.’

01:15:58.520 --> 01:16:03.440
If you think of the London story massively
oversimplified, we have significant number

01:16:03.440 --> 01:16:09.320
of over-target CCGs in the centre of London
for a variety of reasons, a lot of under-target

01:16:09.320 --> 01:16:10.990
CCGs in the outer ring of London.

01:16:10.990 --> 01:16:15.840
It is entirely conceivable that parts of London,
or indeed London as a whole, could choose

01:16:15.840 --> 01:16:19.420
to try and go faster to balance the financial
flows there.

01:16:19.420 --> 01:16:23.560
It is equally possible that Manchester – I
am only picking on devolution places, because

01:16:23.560 --> 01:16:27.380
they are already starting the thinking about
how to work together in that way.

01:16:27.380 --> 01:16:32.040
It is something we would want to facilitate
where it is coming up organically from the

01:16:32.040 --> 01:16:33.830
local health economies concerned.

01:16:33.830 --> 01:16:38.341
It is not something we are planning to impose
on anyone, because by definition it needs

01:16:38.341 --> 01:16:45.760
to be a consensual process.

01:16:45.760 --> 01:16:49.610
I think this is great, but it is also only
half the story.

01:16:49.610 --> 01:16:55.770
What are the other critical success factors
we need to get to where we need to get to?

01:16:55.770 --> 01:17:00.270
We need people who really understand their
populations and the evidence; people who have

01:17:00.270 --> 01:17:04.490
a really good vision of where they want to
take things, with skills being built into

01:17:04.490 --> 01:17:10.100
the health economies that can do that and
really engage clinicians and staff across

01:17:10.100 --> 01:17:11.110
the NHS.

01:17:11.110 --> 01:17:16.560
I think this is fabulous – a great piece
of work that moves us in the right direction

01:17:16.560 --> 01:17:21.670
– but I still have this ‘better sailing
ship’ kind of worry that actually it is

01:17:21.670 --> 01:17:23.520
only half the story.

01:17:23.520 --> 01:17:27.310
In saying that this is brilliant, which is
probably what I really want to say, let us

01:17:27.310 --> 01:17:29.780
not forget the other bit as well.

01:17:29.780 --> 01:17:36.970
Deeply impressive presentations of all the
formulas.

01:17:36.970 --> 01:17:43.400
I think the last point about how this allocation
really drives the right set of behaviours

01:17:43.400 --> 01:17:48.330
in the system, either convening – which
is basically what we are going to have to

01:17:48.330 --> 01:17:55.230
do to get place-based health correct – or
driving outcomes that make sense to people;

01:17:55.230 --> 01:17:59.580
that kind of makes sense to me.

01:17:59.580 --> 01:18:05.100
The thing that has been going round in my
mind is that it is not necessarily the impact

01:18:05.100 --> 01:18:14.020
of any one formula on an area; it is our understanding
of the combinations of all the things we have

01:18:14.020 --> 01:18:16.280
agreed in a particular area.

01:18:16.280 --> 01:18:22.710
Now, I am not asking you that because I think
you have an answer to it; I do not think you

01:18:22.710 --> 01:18:23.710
do.

01:18:23.710 --> 01:18:26.580
But it is a challenge, is it not?

01:18:26.580 --> 01:18:32.640
If you look at the deprivation formula, it
makes sense and moves in the right direction.

01:18:32.640 --> 01:18:38.060
However, then there is another set of applications
of formulas that will impact on the same area,

01:18:38.060 --> 01:18:40.130
and we do not know how.

01:18:40.130 --> 01:18:46.700
I felt the need to say it, because I think
it is the truth of the matter, if you see

01:18:46.700 --> 01:18:52.540
what I mean, and it is a challenge to us in
understanding the combinations, rather than

01:18:52.540 --> 01:18:56.810
the individual impact, as brilliantly as they
have been formulated – and it really is

01:18:56.810 --> 01:18:57.810
brilliant.

01:18:57.810 --> 01:19:02.780
How very kind, and I take the challenge as
well, which is as much a challenge for me

01:19:02.780 --> 01:19:04.020
as anyone else.

01:19:04.020 --> 01:19:07.130
I do not think it is just a challenge for
you, by the way.

01:19:07.130 --> 01:19:09.650
I think it is a challenge for NHS England,
frankly.

01:19:09.650 --> 01:19:10.650
Right.

01:19:10.650 --> 01:19:18.040
Can I just check that the Board now feels
it is in a position where it is confident

01:19:18.040 --> 01:19:20.810
to take the decisions that we are being asked
to take?

01:19:20.810 --> 01:19:24.240
First of all, just picking on that point,
understanding that what we are doing is a

01:19:24.240 --> 01:19:30.300
huge part of providing an improvement in the
quality of healthcare for the population,

01:19:30.300 --> 01:19:32.710
but it is a part.

01:19:32.710 --> 01:19:37.250
Decisions taken around social care and public
health – indeed, across all parts of government

01:19:37.250 --> 01:19:39.930
– are also major contributors.

01:19:39.930 --> 01:19:47.740
Secondly, to confirm with everybody that what
we are doing is committing this organisation

01:19:47.740 --> 01:19:51.580
for five years, subject to the reservations
in annex A.

01:19:51.580 --> 01:19:56.652
You need to feel absolutely comfortable that
the paper, and the explanation that we have

01:19:56.652 --> 01:20:01.140
had from Paul, take us to a place where we
are confident to take those decisions.

01:20:01.140 --> 01:20:09.400
Thirdly, that it is indeed, albeit at a high
level, the best way that we can conceive of

01:20:09.400 --> 01:20:16.530
to drive forward the principles of the Forward
View and get maximum value for patients, plus

01:20:16.530 --> 01:20:22.450
fairness in redressing some historical distortions
in the way that allocations have been made.

01:20:22.450 --> 01:20:28.410
Subject to those overarching criteria, can
I please put to you the following questions?

01:20:28.410 --> 01:20:35.090
First, to ask you to agree the proposed allocation
of funds between areas of commissioning spend,

01:20:35.090 --> 01:20:38.560
including in that the establishment of the
Sustainability and Transformation Fund, and

01:20:38.560 --> 01:20:46.500
including within that a £450 million allocation
to greater Manchester.

01:20:46.500 --> 01:20:51.490
Please can I ask you first to signal your
approval of that?

01:20:51.490 --> 01:20:53.020
Thank you.

01:20:53.020 --> 01:20:58.850
Secondly, to agree the proposed approach to
allocation within CCG, primary care and specialised

01:20:58.850 --> 01:21:01.210
commissioning streams.

01:21:01.210 --> 01:21:02.900
We are all agreed on that?

01:21:02.900 --> 01:21:03.900
Thank you.

01:21:03.900 --> 01:21:09.280
Thirdly, to approve the proposed approach
to pace of change, and to put specifically

01:21:09.280 --> 01:21:14.650
to you option one, which is the option recommended
to us by the team.

01:21:14.650 --> 01:21:17.140
Good, thank you very much.

01:21:17.140 --> 01:21:22.870
Finally, to agree the proposals set out in
paragraphs 60 to 62 with regard to integrated

01:21:22.870 --> 01:21:26.770
planning etc. that Paul has just explained
to us.

01:21:26.770 --> 01:21:27.770
Thank you very much.

01:21:27.770 --> 01:21:34.080
Just to emphasise for everybody, this is a
very high-level exercise.

01:21:34.080 --> 01:21:40.460
The Board has deliberately not been sighted
on what the changes would be in allocations

01:21:40.460 --> 01:21:42.190
to individual CCGs.

01:21:42.190 --> 01:21:49.000
I think it is important to stress that this
has been done entirely at the high level.

01:21:49.000 --> 01:21:55.900
Secondly, I ask you to preserve back a very
residual discretion to the Chief Executive

01:21:55.900 --> 01:22:00.540
and to me as Chairman in governance terms,
should we need to make any minor alterations.

01:22:00.540 --> 01:22:05.790
At this moment, I do not anticipate that it
will be necessary, but please, if you are

01:22:05.790 --> 01:22:07.080
comfortable to do that.

01:22:07.080 --> 01:22:11.650
Thirdly, to understand that the decisions
that we have now taken provide the authority

01:22:11.650 --> 01:22:16.350
necessary for the team to proceed with the
individual CCG-level allocations.

01:22:16.350 --> 01:22:19.350
Can I just check everybody is content with
that?

01:22:19.350 --> 01:22:26.450
Well, it falls to me on the back of all of
that only to repeat again our warmest thanks

01:22:26.450 --> 01:22:27.910
to the team.

01:22:27.910 --> 01:22:31.600
What may not be generally understood is the
amount of pressure under which this has been

01:22:31.600 --> 01:22:38.280
undertaken, given the proximity of the Spending
Review and the long hours and the hard work

01:22:38.280 --> 01:22:39.870
that have been put into it.

01:22:39.870 --> 01:22:45.080
I think I particularly recognise Sam, Steven
and John, who are with us today as our emergency

01:22:45.080 --> 01:22:47.910
back up should anything fail in our major
power supply, i.e.

01:22:47.910 --> 01:22:48.910
Paul.

01:22:48.910 --> 01:22:56.920
I ask you, Paul, through those three, to convey
our gratitude to everybody else who has put

01:22:56.920 --> 01:22:58.140
so much effort into it.

01:22:58.140 --> 01:23:04.170
Our understanding is also that we will only
deliver all of this in partnership with the

01:23:04.170 --> 01:23:06.900
other arm’s-length bodies, the Department
of Health and everybody else who is engaged

01:23:06.900 --> 01:23:09.500
in the exercise.

01:23:09.500 --> 01:23:10.970
Thank you to you personally, Paul, and to
the team.

01:23:10.970 --> 01:23:11.020
Thank you.

